et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). The particular carbonyl assignments here are mechanistic precursor assignments, not a claim that those studies quantified every listed carbonyl. Branched-aldehyde production remains poorly resolved. Reducing local atmospheres are required for useful yields; CO2-rich, hydrogen-poor mixtures are much less productive (Schlesinger and Miller, 1983).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to acetone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 300 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated acetone/cyanide solution with controlled mildly alkaline speciation; cyanohydrin formation is reversible. Ambient-temperature laboratory chemistry is being mapped to a surface pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the acetone carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form the cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an experimentally grounded entry into the acetone-derived valine-precursor branch (Patel et al., 2015). It supplies a C4 skeleton without invoking the undemonstrated acetolactate pathway. Acetone and cyanide availability, dilution, and competing cyanide reactions remain boundary constraints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 300 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"A locally sulfide-rich solution with substantial HS- activity; reagent delivery is staged rather than assumed compatible with every other network reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers, generates an alpha-hydroxythioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied account of Patel et al. (2015) identifies this sulfur-containing intermediate in the acetone homologation branch. Sulfide is a consumed reagent, not an arbitrarily assigned mineral catalyst. Geochemical concentrations sufficient for this branch cannot be assumed from ordinary volcanic outgassing (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical deoxygenative homologation to the isobutyraldehyde level\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 300 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper cyanide photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation, represented by the laboratory 254 nm source; sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. This edge lumps photochemical transformations and aqueous unmasking. Free aldehyde can be transient and immediately trapped by cyanide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox chemistry removes the alpha hydroxyl functionality and reduces the nitrile-derived carbon to the aldehyde oxidation level. Ammonia and elemental sulfur close a formal net redox balance; sulfur speciation and transient intermediates are not resolved by this lumped edge.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the staged sequence reaching a valine aminonitrile precursor, not a demonstrated single-step isolation of free isobutyraldehyde under natural conditions. S8 is an idealized terminal sulfur product for bookkeeping; actual sulfur and polysulfide products require measurement. Unknown cumulative yield, copper speciation, sulfide availability, and operator staging limit geochemical confidence (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker capture of isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous reactants, approximately pH 8-10; ammonia/cyanide ratio controls competition with cyanohydrin formation. Conditions are a plausible reaction window rather than a claimed optimum.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with the aldehyde through carbinolamine/imine intermediates; cyanide addition yields the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The valine-specific Strecker end-game is supported by the supplied literature, including cyanide-release aerosol experiments (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The principal limitations are aldehyde supply and concentration, not the identity of the product skeleton. Primitive-ocean cyanide estimates are too dilute to assume efficient homogeneous synthesis without local accumulation (Stribling and Miller, 1987).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide-rich aqueous capture before substantial ammonia addition; reversible equilibrium, not irreversible storage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the aldehyde produces the C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin trapping is part of the reported acetone-to-valine-aminonitrile sequence (Patel et al., 2015). This alternative explicitly represents cyanide-first reagent ordering. It shares equilibrium chemistry with direct Strecker synthesis and therefore is not an independent origin-of-life framework.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-mediated conversion of cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia addition after cyanohydrin formation; mildly alkaline solution with sufficient free ammonia.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation permits aldehyde/imine formation and cyanide recapture as the aminonitrile; this is not assumed to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Addition of ammonia to the relevant cyanosulfidic intermediate gives the valine aminonitrile in the supplied account of Patel et al. (2015). Natural equivalents of staged ammonia delivery and a substrate-specific cumulative yield remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of valine aminonitrile to valinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for general-acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K, proposed geochemical hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3 in an acidic geothermal pool, after nitrile formation. The temperature/pH window is proposed, not a valine-specific kinetic measurement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition and tautomerization to the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This resolves the first chemical stage of the established aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis end-game discussed for prebiotic Strecker chemistry (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The supplied evidence does not establish fast hydrolysis under these exact natural conditions. Amide accumulation and competition with precursor decomposition are explicit kinetic uncertainties.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Valinamide hydrolysis to racemic valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for general-acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed geochemical hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence may be required. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions, and valine speciation changes upon subsequent neutralization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the activated amide and C-N bond cleavage release the amino acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis completes the valine Strecker sequence (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The reaction is chemically established, but the supplied literature does not establish its rate or yield in a mildly heated natural pool. Laboratory acid hydrolysis must not be conflated with demonstrated environmental production of free valine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate aldehyde Bucherer-Bergs synthesis of valine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K, proposed substrate-screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated cyanide and ammonia with dissolved CO2/bicarbonate/carbonate, approximately pH 8-10. Substrate-specific prebiotic conditions are not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbonyl amination/cyanide addition, carbamylation, and cyclization produce 5-isopropylhydantoin rather than proceeding directly through free aminonitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as an isobutyraldehyde-specific prebiotic pathway in the supplied evidence. The underlying Bucherer-Bergs reaction class is established, while the provided modern demonstration concerns alpha-ketoacid systems (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This chemically distinct trapping option is included conditionally; no valine yield or natural-pool demonstration is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Valine hydantoin ring opening\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for acid-promoted hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K, proposed hydrolysis range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic geothermal processing, approximately pH 1-3. These are candidate conditions rather than conditions verified for this hydantoin.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of a hydantoin ring amide bond produces N-carbamoylvaline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for this substrate and environmental window. Hydantoin-to-amino-acid chemistry is supported at the reaction-class level by Pulletikurti et al. (2022), but the reported slow hydrolysis of other substrates warns against assuming rapid release of valine. This edge explicitly represents a likely bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic deprotection of N-carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for acid-promoted hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K, proposed hydrolysis range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic aqueous heating; incomplete conversion and long residence times are possible. No enzyme or activated biological cofactor is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl group and decomposition of the resulting carbamic-acid fragment release valine, ammonia, and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a separately resolved valine-specific prebiotic step. It is the chemically required deprotection stage of the hydantoin alternative, informed by the broader hydantoin hydrolysis chemistry in Pulletikurti et al. (2022). Neither quantitative valine release nor environmental compatibility follows from that analogy alone.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical reductive carboxylation of isobutyric acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K proposed screening window; no demonstrated conditions\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Elevated H2/CO2 availability; required pressures are unknown\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Explicit missing-chemistry edge, not an executable protocol. No effective nonenzymatic catalyst, activation chemistry, rate, or yield is established. Null catalyst denotes unidentified catalysis, not evidence that catalysis is unnecessary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal reductive incorporation of CO2 converts the C4 carboxylic acid skeleton into a C5 alpha-ketoacid. An actual mechanism would require overcoming difficult carboxylate activation and C-C bond-formation barriers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Keefe, Lazcano and Miller (1995) proposed reductive carboxylation of branched fatty acids as an evolutionary alternative, but the supplied review identifies no nonenzymatic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis. This gap is included to expose, rather than conceal, the upstream dependence of reductive-amination pathways. General hydrothermal thermodynamic favorability does not establish this reaction's kinetics or mechanism (Amend and Shock, 1998).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Metallic nickel supported on silica-alumina, laboratory catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, approximately 10 mol% Ni, 72 h. A cooled pressurized pore or compartment is an environmental analogy, not a demonstrated field setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/iminium-equivalent intermediate with the ketoacid; nickel activates H2 and hydrogenates it to valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine formation from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024), with no reduction in the catalyst-free control. Supported laboratory nickel cannot simply be equated with any nickel-bearing mineral. Ketoacid supply, catalyst accessibility, and competing hydroxyacid formation remain limitations; sulfide and cyanide may also poison the metal surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Palladium on carbon, laboratory catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, approximately 1 mol% Pd, 72 h.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Palladium-mediated H2 activation reduces the ammonia-derived imine to valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly support the palladium-catalyzed end-game and report generally improved amino-acid selectivity across the substrate panel. Panel-wide yield ranges are not assigned here as valine-specific yields. Pd/C is an experimental benchmark with weak geochemical availability, not an assumed abundant early-Earth mineral.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing Fe-Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Exposed nickel-bearing meteoritic metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h, and 3 mg ground Aletai meteorite, corresponding to approximately 18 mol% Ni in the experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Accessible meteoritic metal sites activate hydrogen and reduce the ketoacid-derived imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a valine-relevant tested catalyst material rather than a generic appeal to iron minerals (Kaur et al., 2024). Grinding exposes fresh surface in the laboratory; natural surface accessibility, passivation, local particle abundance, and competition from carbonyl reduction must be assessed independently.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to hydroxyisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing Fe-Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Exposed nickel-bearing meteoritic metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"A competing branch under the reductive-amination conditions; it does not require ammonia consumption.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-activated hydrogen reduces the keto carbonyl directly, bypassing nitrogen incorporation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxyacid formation is an experimentally observed competitor in the nickel and meteorite systems of Kaur et al. (2024). This sink prevents interpreting ketoacid disappearance as valine yield. The study's approximate panel-wide amino/hydroxy product ratios are not imposed as a valine-specific branching ratio.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate carboxylated hydantoin formation from alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300 K, proposed substrate-screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia/cyanide chemistry in bicarbonate-containing solution. The exact intermediate and conditions require validation for alpha-ketoisovalerate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the keto group formally gives a hydantoin bearing both an isopropyl substituent and the original carboxyl group at its substituted ring carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for this substrate and explicitly drawn intermediate. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) support cyanide/ammonia conversion of alpha-ketoacids through hydantoin-related chemistry, but the supplied findings do not establish this alpha-ketoisovalerate intermediate. Isotope labeling would be needed to verify carbon mapping and distinguish this proposal from alternative cyanide-mediated mechanisms.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate decarboxylation of carboxylated valine hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K, proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed thermal/proton-transfer-assisted decarboxylation; no substrate-specific rate or environmental demonstration is available.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of the ring-attached carboxyl group as CO2, followed by proton transfer, yields 5-isopropylhydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative mechanistic completion of the candidate ketoacid Bucherer-Bergs branch. The broader ketoacid-to-amino-acid chemistry is informed by Pulletikurti et al. (2022), but this edge is not claimed as an independently demonstrated valine-specific transformation. It exposes the required carbon-loss step instead of incorrectly treating a carboxylated hydantoin as ordinary valine hydantoin.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing formaldehyde-isobutyraldehyde aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved orthophosphate for general acid-base catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Orthophosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral phosphate-containing solution. Formaldehyde availability determines the extent of diversion from valine precursors.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Phosphate-assisted enolization of isobutyraldehyde enables aldol addition to formaldehyde, giving hydroxypivaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol between these aldehydes is supported by Fairchild et al. (2024). This is a chemically required competing branch when formaldehyde accompanies discharge-derived isobutyraldehyde; it is not added merely to increase network connectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation on hydroxypivaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous cyanide capture of the aldol-derived aldehyde; competing ammonia chemistry is possible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the aldehyde produces the nitrile precursor of pantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This resolves the expected cyanohydrin stage in pantoic-acid formation from isobutyraldehyde, formaldehyde, and HCN (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993). The supplied findings support the overall branch, not a separately quantified yield for this intermediate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of pantoic acid nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for general-acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K, proposed hydrolysis range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous heating after cyanohydrin capture; this net reaction includes the unlisted amide stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Successive nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis produce pantoic acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pantoic-acid production by this precursor combination is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993). The detailed hydrolysis window here is a geochemical extrapolation, not a reported substrate-specific kinetic result. This branch consumes both the branched aldehyde resource and cyanide.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Pantoic acid lactonization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons for acid-catalyzed esterification\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic concentrating films or wet-dry cycling; water removal favors lactone formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular esterification between the terminal hydroxyl and carboxyl group forms a five-membered lactone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The pantoate/pantolactone branch is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993) and contextualized by the related precursor chemistry in Fairchild et al. (2024). Pantolactone is an off-path product here, not an intermediate in valine synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_024\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing butanal Strecker sequence to norvaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_028\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons during the later hydrolysis stage\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K capture, followed by 340-370 K proposed hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons during hydrolysis only\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Lumped, staged Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis. Ammonia appears on both sides because imine-forming nitrogen is incorporated while nitrile nitrogen is released during hydrolysis; isotope identities are not encoded.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Butanal forms an aminonitrile with ammonia and cyanide; hydrolysis gives the straight-chain C5 amino acid rather than valine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Norvaline accompanies valine in discharge products (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). The butanal assignment is the conventional Strecker precursor mapping, not proof that every discharge-derived norvaline molecule followed this resolved mechanism. Chromatographic distinction from valine and isovaline is essential.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_025\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing butan-2-one Strecker sequence to isovaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous protons during the later hydrolysis stage\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K capture, followed by 340-370 K proposed hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous protons during hydrolysis only\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Lumped, staged ketone Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis. Capture and hydrolysis need not have the same pH or occur in the same pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ketimine formation and cyanide addition generate an alpha-methyl aminonitrile; hydrolysis produces isovaline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Isovaline is a documented coproduct of the discharge systems (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). Butan-2-one explains its carbon skeleton through established Strecker chemistry, but this precursor assignment is not a quantitative reconstruction of discharge mechanisms. It is explicitly distinguished from valine, which has an alpha hydrogen.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-derived aldehyde with direct Strecker processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Discharge supplies a mixed carbonyl pool, followed by direct ammonia/cyanide capture of isobutyraldehyde and stepwise nitrile hydrolysis. Valine production by the overall experimental platform is established; the resolved upstream aldehyde flux and natural hydrolysis kinetics are not.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-derived aldehyde with cyanohydrin-first Strecker processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The discharge-derived aldehyde is first trapped with cyanide, then exposed to ammonia before hydrolysis. This is a distinct reagent-ordering sequence within Strecker chemistry, not a separate independently demonstrated mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-derived aldehyde with conditional hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Isobutyraldehyde from the discharge precursor pool is directed into a candidate Bucherer-Bergs branch. Hydantoin formation and environmentally effective hydrolysis require substrate-specific validation; this is not a demonstrated complete prebiotic route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic homologation with direct Strecker capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone undergoes cyanide addition, sulfur chemistry, and photochemical homologation. The aldehyde-level product is then directly captured with ammonia and cyanide. Free-aldehyde release and capture are a lumped interpretation of staged chemistry, not a demonstrated autonomous pool sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic homologation with cyanohydrin-first capture\",\n      \"description\": \"This sequence most closely follows the supplied staged acetone-to-valine-aminonitrile evidence: cyanohydrin formation, sulfur-mediated photochemistry, further cyanohydrin capture, and ammonia addition. Hydrolysis extends the demonstrated precursor synthesis to free valine, with environmental kinetics unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanosulfidic homologation with conditional hydantoin capture\",\n      \"description\": \"The acetone-derived aldehyde-level product is hypothetically redirected into hydantoin chemistry. This combines supported upstream reaction classes with an unvalidated valine-specific hydantoin branch and should be treated as an experimental hypothesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical fatty-acid carboxylation followed by nickel reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative isobutyrate-to-alpha-ketoisovalerate step supplies the substrate for experimentally supported nickel-catalyzed valine formation. The complete pathway is unestablished because its skeleton-building reaction lacks a demonstrated catalyst or mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical fatty-acid carboxylation followed by palladium reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"This shares the unresolved ketoacid-supply reaction with P7 but uses the tested Pd/C end-game. It is a catalyst-specific alternative rather than a new carbon-skeleton pathway; palladium availability further weakens geochemical plausibility.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical fatty-acid carboxylation followed by meteoritic-metal reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative ketoacid supply is followed by the demonstrated ground-iron-meteorite amination chemistry. This tests a naturally occurring catalytic material, but does not solve precursor supply, fresh-surface availability, or hydroxyacid competition.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical fatty-acid carboxylation followed by ketoacid hydantoin processing\",\n      \"description\": \"This is the most conditional sequence: unestablished alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis is followed by a proposed carboxylated-hydantoin/decarboxylation mechanism and hydrolysis. It exposes the necessary carbon accounting and experimental gaps rather than claiming a demonstrated tenth independent synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n 