-2-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Isovaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Discharge-driven supply of cyanide and mixed carbonyl precursors", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_023", "mol_028", "mol_029", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk gas and collection water; discharge channels are transiently much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Electrical discharge through an anoxic, locally reducing methane/ammonia/water-vapor mixture, followed by aqueous collection. This is a lumped precursor-supply process, not an elementary reaction or an exhaustive product inventory."}, "mechanism": "Energetic fragmentation and radical recombination create cyanide and mixed carbon skeletons; oxygenated products are collected in water. Hydrogen represents one possible reduced coproduct.", "reasoning": "Discharge experiments establish production of valine together with norvaline and isovaline (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). The particular carbonyl assignments here are mechanistic precursor assignments, not a claim that those studies quantified every listed carbonyl. Branched-aldehyde production remains poorly resolved. Reducing local atmospheres are required for useful yields; CO2-rich, hydrogen-poor mixtures are much less productive (Schlesinger and Miller, 1983)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide addition to acetone", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 300 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated acetone/cyanide solution with controlled mildly alkaline speciation; cyanohydrin formation is reversible. Ambient-temperature laboratory chemistry is being mapped to a surface pool."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the acetone carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form the cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "This is an experimentally grounded entry into the acetone-derived valine-precursor branch (Patel et al., 2015). It supplies a C4 skeleton without invoking the undemonstrated acetolactate pathway. Acetone and cyanide availability, dilution, and competing cyanide reactions remain boundary constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 300 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "A locally sulfide-rich solution with substantial HS- activity; reagent delivery is staged rather than assumed compatible with every other network reaction."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide addition to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers, generates an alpha-hydroxythioamide.", "reasoning": "The supplied account of Patel et al. (2015) identifies this sulfur-containing intermediate in the acetone homologation branch. Sulfide is a consumed reagent, not an arbitrarily assigned mineral catalyst. Geochemical concentrations sufficient for this branch cannot be assumed from ordinary volcanic outgassing (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Photochemical deoxygenative homologation to the isobutyraldehyde level", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 300 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper cyanide photoredox species", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation, represented by the laboratory 254 nm source; sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. This edge lumps photochemical transformations and aqueous unmasking. Free aldehyde can be transient and immediately trapped by cyanide."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox chemistry removes the alpha hydroxyl functionality and reduces the nitrile-derived carbon to the aldehyde oxidation level. Ammonia and elemental sulfur close a formal net redox balance; sulfur speciation and transient intermediates are not resolved by this lumped edge.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support the staged sequence reaching a valine aminonitrile precursor, not a demonstrated single-step isolation of free isobutyraldehyde under natural conditions. S8 is an idealized terminal sulfur product for bookkeeping; actual sulfur and polysulfide products require measurement. Unknown cumulative yield, copper speciation, sulfide availability, and operator staging limit geochemical confidence (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Direct Strecker capture of isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated aqueous reactants, approximately pH 8-10; ammonia/cyanide ratio controls competition with cyanohydrin formation. Conditions are a plausible reaction window rather than a claimed optimum."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condenses with the aldehyde through carbinolamine/imine intermediates; cyanide addition yields the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "The valine-specific Strecker end-game is supported by the supplied literature, including cyanide-release aerosol experiments (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The principal limitations are aldehyde supply and concentration, not the identity of the product skeleton. Primitive-ocean cyanide estimates are too dilute to assume efficient homogeneous synthesis without local accumulation (Stribling and Miller, 1987)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanide-rich aqueous capture before substantial ammonia addition; reversible equilibrium, not irreversible storage."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the aldehyde produces the C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin trapping is part of the reported acetone-to-valine-aminonitrile sequence (Patel et al., 2015). This alternative explicitly represents cyanide-first reagent ordering. It shares equilibrium chemistry with direct Strecker synthesis and therefore is not an independent origin-of-life framework."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ammonia-mediated conversion of cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia addition after cyanohydrin formation; mildly alkaline solution with sufficient free ammonia."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation permits aldehyde/imine formation and cyanide recapture as the aminonitrile; this is not assumed to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Addition of ammonia to the relevant cyanosulfidic intermediate gives the valine aminonitrile in the supplied account of Patel et al. (2015). Natural equivalents of staged ammonia delivery and a substrate-specific cumulative yield remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydration of valine aminonitrile to valinamide", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous protons for general-acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K, proposed geochemical hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous protons", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3 in an acidic geothermal pool, after nitrile formation. The temperature/pH window is proposed, not a valine-specific kinetic measurement."}, "mechanism": "Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition and tautomerization to the amide.", "reasoning": "This resolves the first chemical stage of the established aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis end-game discussed for prebiotic Strecker chemistry (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The supplied evidence does not establish fast hydrolysis under these exact natural conditions. Amide accumulation and competition with precursor decomposition are explicit kinetic uncertainties."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Valinamide hydrolysis to racemic valine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous protons for general-acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed geochemical hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous protons", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-3; prolonged residence may be required. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions, and valine speciation changes upon subsequent neutralization."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to the activated amide and C-N bond cleavage release the amino acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis completes the valine Strecker sequence (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013). The reaction is chemically established, but the supplied literature does not establish its rate or yield in a mildly heated natural pool. Laboratory acid hydrolysis must not be conflated with demonstrated environmental production of free valine."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Candidate aldehyde Bucherer-Bergs synthesis of valine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003", "mol_002", "mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K, proposed substrate