dium-catalyzed end-game and report generally improved amino-acid selectivity across the substrate panel. Panel-wide yield ranges are not assigned here as valine-specific yields. Pd/C is an experimental benchmark with weak geochemical availability, not an assumed abundant early-Earth mineral."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing Fe-Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Exposed nickel-bearing meteoritic metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h, and 3 mg ground Aletai meteorite, corresponding to approximately 18 mol% Ni in the experiment."}, "mechanism": "Accessible meteoritic metal sites activate hydrogen and reduce the ketoacid-derived imine.", "reasoning": "This is a valine-relevant tested catalyst material rather than a generic appeal to iron minerals (Kaur et al., 2024). Grinding exposes fresh surface in the laboratory; natural surface accessibility, passivation, local particle abundance, and competition from carbonyl reduction must be assessed independently."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to hydroxyisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite containing Fe-Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Exposed nickel-bearing meteoritic metal", "aqueous": true, "other": "A competing branch under the reductive-amination conditions; it does not require ammonia consumption."}, "mechanism": "Metal-activated hydrogen reduces the keto carbonyl directly, bypassing nitrogen incorporation.", "reasoning": "Hydroxyacid formation is an experimentally o