nvironmental_progression\": 0.4,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.3,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.32,\n      \"feedback\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is strongly supported by Kaur et al. (2024). The complete pathway is nevertheless limited by rxn_013: no demonstrated abiotic activation/catalytic mechanism is provided for reductive carboxylation of unactivated isobutyric acid. Its balanced equation and hypothetical label do not establish kinetic feasibility. 'Biochemical' also does not specify the physical setting needed for the experimentally supported final reaction.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_014\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Treat rxn_013 as an experimental hypothesis until alpha-ketoisovalerate formation is demonstrated with a defined catalyst, activation chemistry, and carbon-source tracing.\",\n        \"Specify the rxn_014 benchmark conditions: approximately 22 degrees C, pH 8, 5 bar H2, 72 hours, ammonia concentration, and Ni/support loading.\",\n        \"Justify catalyst availability, cooling/transfer from the hydrothermal setting, and retention of the required hydrogen supply.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.94\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.44,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.4,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.34,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.3,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.31,\n      \"feedback\": \"The palladium-catalyzed final reaction is experimentally well supported, but improved downstream catalysis does not resolve the absent alpha-ketoisovalerate supply mechanism. The environmental limitation is not palladium chemistry itself: the graph provides no local source, catalyst form, support, or loading corresponding to the Pd/C laboratory demonstration.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_015\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Validate rxn_013 independently before counting this as a demonstrated feedstock-to-valine pathway.\",\n        \"Distinguish laboratory Pd/C from any proposed natural catalyst and document availability, loading, hydrogen pressure, pH, and reaction time.\",\n        \"Quantify amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid production under the specific conditions used rather than importing selectivity from other substrates.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.15,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.42,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.4,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.39,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.3,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.32,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) supports reductive amination using ground iron meteorite, so the final chemistry is credible without enzymes. The upstream reductive carboxylation remains unsupported, however, and meteorite occurrence alone does not establish the required reactive surface area, metal exposure, hydrogen supply, or catalyst persistence. The included hydroxy-acid branch appropriately acknowledges competing reduction.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate alpha-ketoisovalerate production from isobutyrate and CO2 before claiming complete pathway operation.\",\n        \"Specify meteorite composition, preparation, exposed surface area, catalyst loading, corrosion state, and the aqueous hydrogen/ammonia conditions.\",\n        \"Measure valine and alpha-hydroxyisovalerate together to determine the actual branching fraction.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.15,\n       