the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: The network is not approved at the network-level threshold of 0.9. Structural validity is high: the 31 molecule nodes and 25 reaction nodes form a connected graph, identifiers are consistent, and the target and major intermediates are appropriately distinguished. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, every reaction conserves atoms and charge with suitable coefficients; there are no conservation violations. Record the inferred coefficients explicitly for rxn_001, rxn_004, and rxn_022. The large coefficients in rxn_001 reflect a lumped mixed-product representation, not evidence for measured product ratios or a single mechanism.\n\nThe strongest supported route is P5, with P1, P2, and P4 also providing credible routes to racemic valine once operating conditions are supplied. The speculative alpha-ketoisovalerate branches do not block these alternatives, but cannot increase confidence merely by adding routes. Acetone and isobutyric acid are legitimate scoped boundary inputs; their lack of in-network synthesis is not a structural defect. Their availability and concentration remain environmental assumptions.\n\nThe complete network appropriately includes competing chemistry. rxn_017 is a supported ketoacid-reduction sink. rxn_020-rxn_023 form a chemically plausible, literature-aligned pantoate/pantolactone branch, although aldol catalysis, nitrile hydrolysis, and lactonization conditions need specification. rxn_024-rxn_025 are sensible net Strecker-plus-hydrolysis sequences; ammonia appearing on both sides does not remove the need for an ammonia-containing reaction medium. Mixed C5 amino-acid production is not itself failure to achieve valine, but requires analytical isomer resolution.\n\nEnvironmental consistency is the main network-wide implementation weakness. The supplied reaction nodes give categorical labels rather than temperatures, pressures, pH values, concentrations, catalyst forms, residence times, or transfer protocols. 'Biochemical' is not a defined physicochemical environment and does not establish an enzyme requirement. Missing details should be treated as uncertainty, not proof that all proposed settings are incompatible.\n\nLiterature alignment is good at the strategy level: the network correctly distinguishes demonstrated end-game chemistry, staged aminonitrile synthesis, conditional hydantoin routes, and speculative carboxylation. It also acknowledges important competing sinks and supply limitations. However, reaction-level citations and operational reasoning are absent. Attach primary evidence to the relevant nodes and distinguish observations from extrapolations. Problematic reaction lists include these implementation gaps as well as unsupported chemistry; none denotes an atom-balance violation.\n- Issues: ['The proposed aldehyde Bucherer\u2013Bergs route has sensible connectivity and correctly identified hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates. Its conditional designation is appropriate. However, formation of this particular hydantoin under the proposed environmental conditions is not established by the supplied evidence, and ring opening followed by complete decarbamoylation cannot be assumed to proceed efficiently in unspecified surface water.', 'The supported acetone homologation chemistry provides a credible entry to the aldehyde level, but diversion into hydantoin formation and subsequent hydrolysis remains conditional. The graph does not establish that the photochemical product mixture is compatible with hydantoin formation or that the resulting hydantoin and N-carbamoylvaline will release valine at useful rates. These are implementation and evidence gaps, not consequences of the number of stages.', \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is strongly supported by Kaur et al. (2024). The complete pathway is nevertheless limited by rxn_013: no demonstrated abiotic activation/catalytic mechanism is provided for reductive carboxylation of unactivated isobutyric acid. Its balanced equation and hypothetical label do not establish kinetic feasibility. 'Biochemical' also does not specify the physical setting needed for the experimentally supported final reaction.\", 'The palladium-catalyzed final reaction is experimentally well supported, but improved downstream catalysis does not resolve the absent alpha-ketoisovalerate supply mechanism. The environmental limitation is not palladium chemistry itself: the graph provides no local source, catalyst form, support, or loading corresponding to the Pd/C laboratory demonstration.', 'Kaur et al. (2024) supports reductive amination using ground iron meteorite, so the final chemistry is credible without enzymes. The upstream reductive carboxylation remains unsupported, however, and meteorite occurrence alone does not establish the required reactive surface area, metal exposure, hydrogen supply, or catalyst persistence. The included hydroxy-acid branch appropriately acknowledges competing reduction.', 'This pathway combines the unsupported alpha-ketoisovalerate supply step with substrate-specific extrapolations of ketoacid hydantoin chemistry. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) supports the general reaction family, but the supplied evidence does not establish the proposed carboxylated valine hydantoin, its selective decarboxylation, and complete hydrolysis under the modeled conditions. The molecular bookkeeping is consistent; the shortcomings are experimental and mechanistic support.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine, racemic\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This connected network contains 25 molecule nodes, 19 reaction nodes and 10 distinct boundary-to-target reaction sequences. The ten sequences are explicitly route variants, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic origins: they combine alternative aldehyde supplies, Strecker entry chemistry, hydrolysis regimes and three experimentally tested reductive-amination catalysts. The strongest skeleton-building branch is staged acetone cyanosulfidic homologation; discharge chemistry provides a less selective alternative. Acetone, externally supplied isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid are declared boundary feedstocks. In particular, the alpha-ketoisovaleric acid supply remains unresolved: no speculative reductive carboxylation or nonenzymatic acetolactate synthesis is represented as established chemistry. Boundary supplies can replenish molecules that also function as internal hubs. Reaction conditions distinguish reported experimental benchmarks from proposed environmental implementation windows. Photochemistry, ammonia addition, nitrile hydrolysis and metal hydrogenation require separate stages; sulfide/cyanide-rich mixtures are not assumed compatible with hydrogenation catalysts. All settings are anoxic. Neutral molecular formulas provide acid-base bookkeeping; actual protonation follows the stated pH. The environment_formed field identifies the principal formation environment where multiple environments apply. Non-target terminal products use the schema's intermediate role because no byproduct role is available. Valine is racemic, competing sinks are explicit, and no cumulative yield or geochemically closed synthesis is claimed.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n