iupac_name\": \"butanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"butyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-2-3-4-5/h4H,2-3H2,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methyl ethyl ketone\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-3-4(2)5/h3H2,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-norvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-2-3-4(6)5(7)8/h4H,2-3,6H2,1H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-2-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-isovaline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3-5(2,6)4(7)8/h3,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous capture of cyanide and carbonyl precursors\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K bulk gas and aqueous trap; discharge channels are transiently much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic methane/ammonia-containing gas, electrical discharge, water-vapor circulation and prompt condensation. Repeated discharge over days is an experimental analogue, not a specified natural production rate. Carbonyl concentrations and their isomer distribution must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A coarse-grained source reaction representing radical chemistry followed by capture, not one elementary reaction. One atom-balanced bookkeeping realization is: 14 methane + ammonia + 4 water -> hydrogen cyanide + isobutyraldehyde + formaldehyde + butanal + butan-2-one + 20 hydrogen. These coefficients are neither measured yields nor predicted product ratios.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine, norvaline and isovaline occur in reducing-gas discharge products (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). Their assignment here to particular carbonyl precursors is a mechanistic network interpretation, not proof that this precursor mixture was isolated in those proportions. This source is conditional on a locally reducing atmosphere or plume; yields from weakly reducing mixtures can be much lower (Schlesinger and Miller, 1983). It should not be interpreted as a selective isobutyraldehyde synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K proposed surface implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline, approximately pH 8-9, with cyanide availability maintained. A 10-100 mM substrate screening range is proposed, not reported as a geochemical concentration or an optimized literature recipe. Limit volatile losses and avoid prolonged heating.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the acetone carbonyl followed by proton transfer produces the C4 cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This entry reaction belongs to the acetone-to-valine-precursor sequence described by Patel et al. (2015). The equilibrium requires sufficient cyanide activity; neither acetone availability nor concentration of cyanide in an ordinary ocean is established. Local concentration or replenishment is necessary, consistent with the dilution problem discussed by Stribling and Miller (1987).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-containing solution; approximately pH 7-9 maintains some nucleophilic HS-. Use a separate sulfide-addition stage. Concentration and residence time require validation against the original experimental protocol; no conversion rate is assumed here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the alpha-hydroxythioamide. Net: cyanohydrin + hydrogen sulfide -> hydroxythioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The sulfur-mediated intermediate is supported by the cyanosulfidic sequence summarized from Patel et al. (2015). Sulfide is a consumed reagent, not a mineral catalyst. Sustained dissolved sulfide is a major environmental constraint: ordinary volcanic outgassing does not necessarily provide the required HS- inventories (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreductive processing to the isobutyraldehyde level\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K proposed surface implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN-derived copper-cyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation near the laboratory 254 nm benchmark, staged sulfide supply and anoxic conditions. Use the demonstrated copper formulation rather than substituting an unspecified copper mineral. Numerical copper loading and irradiation dose are not supplied in the research extract and must be checked in the primary methods. Capture the carbonyl promptly; an accumulating free-aldehyde pool is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Coarse-grained photodeoxygenation, reductive thioamide processing and carbonyl release. A formal balanced closure is: 4 hydroxythioamide + 4 hydrogen sulfide -> 4 isobutyraldehyde + 4 ammonia + cyclooctasulfur. S8 represents an idealized oxidized-sulfur endpoint; the node does not assert measured sulfur speciation or a single elementary step.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports staged access to the valine aminonitrile through this carbonyl level, generally captured downstream as a cyanohydrin. Representing free isobutyraldehyde and S8 explicitly is a reduced-resolution mechanistic model, not a claim that either was isolated in the displayed stoichiometry. Direct diversion of the transient aldehyde into ammonia-first Strecker chemistry is an extrapolation. Sulfide availability, copper speciation, UV spectrum and operator-like staging remain unresolved environmental issues (Ranjan et al., 2018). No cumulative valine yield is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7-9 implementation window. Cyanide is available before substantial ammonia addition. Prompt capture limits aldehyde volatilization and competing aldol reactions; avoid treating a highly dilute ocean as the reaction medium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible nucleophilic addition of cyanide to isobutyraldehyde gives the racemic C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin trapping is part of the demonstrated cyanosulfidic route to the valine aminonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). This branch is chemically distinguished from ammonia-first capture by reagent order, but both access an interconnected carbonyl/cyanohydrin/imine system rather than independent synthesis frameworks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-first Strecker formation of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9, ammonia in excess over aldehyde, and replenished cyanide. Millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar aldehyde is a concentration target for testing, not an established natural inventory. If supplied from photochemistry, ammonia addition follows that stage and requires compatibility testing.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia and aldehyde establish a carbinolamine/imine equilibrium; cyanide addition to the imine or iminium species yields racemic valine aminonitrile and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The isobutyraldehyde Strecker end-game is established, including prebiotic aerosol/cyanide-source variants discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). No mineral catalyst is required. Its principal limitations are aldehyde supply, cyanide dilution and competition from cyanohydrin or formaldehyde-dependent chemistry, not uncertainty about the basic C-N and C-C bond-forming reactions. Capture directly after rxn_004 is a conditional implementation variant rather than the specifically demonstrated staged sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia conversion of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia is introduced after cyanohydrin formation; proposed pH 8-9. Separate this operation from sulfide photochemistry. Maintain ammonia activity and assay residual cyanohydrin rather than assuming quantitative exchange.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net replacement of cyanohydrin hydroxyl by amino functionality proceeds through reversible carbonyl release, imine formation and cyanide recapture; it is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conversion of the corresponding cyanohydrin-level intermediate to the valine aminonitrile by ammonia addition is supported by Patel et al. (2015). The staged sequence is stronger evidence than an assumed one-pot mixture. Its cumulative efficiency and tolerance of a natural photochemical product mixture remain unquantified in the supplied evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K proposed geothermal implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate acidic compartment, proposed pH 1-2, reached only after aminonitrile production. Days-to-weeks residence is a testing window, not a demonstrated completion time. Acidification requires management of residual volatile HCN and H2S. Thermal hydrolysis can occur in shaded subsurface portions of an otherwise UV-exposed surface system.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration to an amide and amide hydrolysis. Net neutral-form bookkeeping: valine aminonitrile + 2 water -> valine + ammonia; ammonia is predominantly ammonium at this pH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis is the established Strecker end-game underlying the valine syntheses reviewed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). The specific dilute-acid, sub-boiling geological window here is an extrapolated implementation, not a reported optimized valine experiment. Complete hydrolysis, competing decomposition and residence time must be measured; observation of an aminonitrile alone does not establish production of free valine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide-rich aqueous medium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Base-promoted nitrile and amide hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 11-12 in a concentrated alkaline geothermal pool, after aminonitrile formation. Sustained alkalinity is required; the immediate product is valinate. Days-to-months is a screening window, not a validated rate. Account for amide accumulation, cyanide loss and degradation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the nitrile gives an amide-level intermediate; further hydrolysis yields valinate and ammonia. Proton equilibration gives the neutral formula represented by mol_014. Net neutral-form bookkeeping uses two water molecules.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydrolysis is established chemistry, but this valine-specific prebiotic operating window is speculative and is not demonstrated in the supplied studies. It is included as an explicitly conditional alternative to acidic Strecker work-up, not as another proven origin of the branched skeleton. The supplied Strecker review supports the general aminonitrile end-game, not the proposed kinetics (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni supported on silica-alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K, reported experimental benchmark\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar hydrogen\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. Physical setting: cool, closed, hydrogen-pressurized aqueous rock pore or experimental analogue; no enzymes are invoked. Keep cyanide and sulfide streams separate because they can poison or alter the catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible ammonia addition and imine formation are followed by Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation. Net: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + ammonia + hydrogen -> valine + water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine formation from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including the absence of reduction without a metal catalyst. Supported Ni is the demonstrated catalyst; replacing it with an unspecified natural mineral is not assumed equivalent. Exposed native Ni on silicate could be an analogue, but its local loading and persistence require evidence. The unresolved ketoacid supply remains a boundary assumption, and carbonyl reduction competes through rxn_013.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Palladium on carbon\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K, reported experimental benchmark\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar hydrogen\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C, 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. A cool pressurized aqueous compartment is required. This is a laboratory-supported catalyst benchmark, not an assertion that naturally occurring Pd/C deposits were available.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Palladium activates hydrogen and reduces the ketoacid-derived imine to racemic valine, releasing water in the overall reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly supports this final transformation and reports improved amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid selectivity with Pd across the tested substrate set. A valine-specific numerical yield is not assigned from pooled ranges. Palladium rarity, its support and reactive surface availability make this a geochemically conditional route despite strong reaction-level evidence; it does not solve alpha-ketoisovalerate supply.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite with exposed nickel-containing metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K, reported experimental benchmark\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar hydrogen\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ground Aletai meteorite, reported loading approximately 18 mol% Ni\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. The reported experiment used 3 mg meteorite; scaling must preserve metal-to-substrate loading. Particle size, aliquot volume and pretreatment must be obtained from the primary methods rather than inferred from meteorite mass alone. A natural analogue requires freshly exposed metal and sustained hydrogen.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reactive metallic sites activate hydrogen and hydrogenate the ammonia-derived imine; competing ketoacid hydrogenation produces hydroxyisovalerate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ground iron-meteorite catalysis of the valine end-game is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024). Meteorite occurrence alone does not guarantee equivalent surface area, exposed Ni, corrosion state or catalytic lifetime. This route retains both the purchased-substrate limitation and the competing reduction sink, without invoking enzymes or an unverified upstream carboxylation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to hydroxyisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or exposed Aletai Fe-Ni metal, used as alternative catalysts\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar hydrogen\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"The Ni or meteorite catalyst specified in rxn_010 or rxn_012\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same pH 8, 30 mM ketoacid, ammonium-containing medium and 72 h benchmark as the corresponding amination branch. This is a competing reaction in those mixtures, not a claim that both alternative catalysts must be combined.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl, rather than its ammonia-derived imine, yields racemic alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reports substantial hydroxy-acid competition with Ni and meteorite catalysts. The network therefore does not treat ketoacid consumption as synonymous with valine yield or assign all hydrogenation flux to amination. Exact valine-specific branching fractions are not supplied by the research summary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-dependent diversion of isobutyraldehyde by aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous inorganic phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Phosphate-mediated general acid-base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral pH, approximately 6.5-7.5, with both aldehydes and dissolved phosphate present. Reactant concentrations, phosphate loading and residence time should follow the primary aldol experiments before quantitative competition with Strecker chemistry is inferred.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization of isobutyraldehyde followed by addition to formaldehyde produces hydroxypivaldehyde, consuming the branched aldehyde otherwise available for valine synthesis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The neutral-pH, phosphate-catalyzed aldol diversion is supported by Fairchild et al. (2024). Phosphate is included for a specific catalytic function, not merely because it is a common prebiotic mineral constituent. Its effect on valine yield depends on formaldehyde concentration and reaction timing; complete diversion is not assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation on hydroxypivaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7-9 with cyanide available and ammonia low enough that the cyanohydrin branch is competitive. This operation follows aldol addition; the branch fraction is not specified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the remaining aldehyde carbonyl, forming the nitrile precursor of pantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pantoic-acid production from isobutyraldehyde, formaldehyde and cyanide is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993), while Fairchild et al. (2024) supports the upstream aldol chemistry. This node is a chemically resolved representation of that diversion, not a measured intermediate yield under the proposed environmental window.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of pantoic acid nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K proposed geothermal implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate acidic stage, proposed pH 1-2. Monitor nitrile and amide disappearance over days to weeks; complete conversion at these conditions is not established by the supplied extract. Residual cyanide must not be assumed compatible with the acidification stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis gives pantoic acid and ammonia. Net: pantoic acid nitrile + 2 water -> pantoic acid + ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall pantoic-acid branch is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993). The proposed geological hydrolysis window is a conditional implementation of established nitrile chemistry, not a validated residence-time prediction. Partial hydrolysis can retain material in nitrile or amide pools, so pantolactone formation is not assumed quantitative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Pantoic acid lactonization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K proposed implementation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic concentrating films or pools, approximately pH 2-4 while wet, followed by partial drying. Reduced water activity favors lactonization; rewetting permits reverse hydrolysis. No fixed conversion or residence time is inferred.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular esterification between the carboxyl group and terminal hydroxyl group forms the five-membered pantolactone ring and releases water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This closes the pantoate/pantolactone sink associated with isobutyraldehyde chemistry (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993; Fairchild et al., 2024). Acid catalysis and water activity are specified because efficient cyclization cannot be assumed in unspecified neutral bulk water. The detailed wet-dry implementation remains an environmental extrapolation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing butanal Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis to norvaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity during the hydrolysis stage only\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K for Strecker capture, then 353-370 K for proposed hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"None for Strecker capture; acid catalysis for subsequent hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sequential ammonia/cyanide-containing medium near pH 8-9, then a separate acidic hydrolysis stage near pH 1-2. Hydrolysis completion at the proposed geothermal conditions must be measured. Resolve the final C5 amino-acid isomers chromatographically.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Coarse-grained imine formation, cyanide addition and aminonitrile hydrolysis. Net: butanal + hydrogen cyanide + ammonia + water -> norvaline + ammonia. Ammonia occurs on both sides because its nitrogen is incorporated while nitrile-derived nitrogen is released; the process is not ammonia-free.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Norvaline is a documented coproduct of valine-producing discharge experiments (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009), and butanal gives its expected Strecker carbon skeleton. This net branch tracks chemical and analytical competition without asserting a measured norvaline/valine ratio or identical rates for the isomeric substrates.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing butan-2-one Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis to isovaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton activity during the hydrolysis stage only\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K for Strecker capture, then 353-370 K for proposed hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"None for Strecker capture; acid catalysis for subsequent hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia/cyanide-containing medium near pH 8-9 followed by separate acidic hydrolysis near pH 1-2. Ketone-derived aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis need not match the aldehyde-derived rates. Confirm isovaline separately from valine and norvaline.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ketimine formation and cyanide addition establish the alpha-methyl aminonitrile skeleton; hydrolysis yields racemic isovaline. Net: butan-2-one + hydrogen cyanide + ammonia + water -> isovaline + ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Isovaline accompanies valine in discharge products (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009), and its relationship to butan-2-one is consistent with Strecker interpretation of meteoritic amino-acid mixtures. This is a competing isomer branch, not evidence for selective valine production. The exact geological hydrolysis schedule remains conditional.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Boundary aldehyde, ammonia-first Strecker, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied isobutyraldehyde enters the established Strecker end-game. Aldehyde availability is a boundary assumption; the proposed geothermal hydrolysis rate is not established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Boundary aldehyde, cyanohydrin-first capture, acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Isobutyraldehyde is captured with cyanide before ammonia addition. This reagent-order variant shares the aminonitrile endpoint with P1 and is not an independent carbon-skeleton origin.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone homologation with ammonia-first interception\",\n      \"description\": \"The supported acetone homologation chemistry supplies a transient aldehyde-level intermediate, followed by direct Strecker interception. Interception before cyanohydrin accumulation is a conditional staging extrapolation, not the specific demonstrated Patel sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Staged cyanosulfidic acetone-to-valine-precursor route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation, sulfur-mediated photoreductive homologation, cyanohydrin capture and ammonia addition follow the strongest supplied evidence for assembling the branched precursor. Hydrolysis extends the demonstrated aminonitrile endpoint to free racemic valine; cumulative yield and natural staging remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-fed ammonia-first Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Reducing-gas discharge supplies a modeled mixture of carbonyls and cyanide; isobutyraldehyde undergoes Strecker capture and acidic hydrolysis. Valine occurrence in discharge experiments is established, but this resolved precursor sequence and its flux are inferred.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-fed cyanohydrin-first Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"The same conditional discharge source feeds cyanohydrin capture before ammonia addition. This is distinct in reaction sequence from P5 but does not constitute another independent discharge framework; isomer production and formaldehyde diversion remain active.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with supported nickel\",\n      \"description\": \"Directly demonstrated reductive-amination endpoint using the reported Ni catalyst and hydrogen conditions. This is boundary-complete from supplied ketoacid, not a demonstrated synthesis of that ketoacid from simple geochemical carbon sources.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with palladium on carbon\",\n      \"description\": \"An independently tested catalyst variant of the reductive-amination end-game. Reaction evidence is strong, but natural Pd/C availability and upstream ketoacid supply are not established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with exposed meteoritic metal\",\n      \"description\": \"An experimentally supported meteorite-catalyzed endpoint. Geological operation additionally requires exposed reactive metal, suitable surface area, sustained hydrogen and a ketoacid inventory; hydroxy-acid competition is represented explicitly.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Boundary aldehyde Strecker route with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A condition-distinct alternative to P1 that avoids acidification but requires prolonged strongly alkaline hydrolysis. Its proposed geological operating window is speculative and must not be counted as a tenth independently validated prebiotic origin.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine, racemic", "overall_strategy": "This connected network contains 25 molecule nodes, 19 reaction nodes and 10 distinct boundary-to-target reaction sequences. The ten sequences are explicitly route variants, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic origins: they combine alternative aldehyde supplies, Strecker entry chemistry, hydrolysis regimes and three experimentally tested reductive-amination catalysts. The strongest skeleton-building branch is staged acetone cyanosulfidic homologation; discharge chemistry provides a less selective alternative. Acetone, externally supplied isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovaleric acid are declared boundary feedstocks. In particular, the alpha-ketoisovaleric acid supply remains unresolved: no speculative reductive carboxylation or nonenzymatic acetolactate synthesis is represented as established chemistry. Boundary supplies can replenish molecules that also function as internal hubs. Reaction conditions distinguish reported experimental benchmarks from proposed environmental implementation windows. Photochemistry, ammonia addition, nitrile hydrolysis and metal hydrogenation require separate stages; sulfide/cyanide-rich mixtures are not assumed compatible with hydrogenation catalysts. All settings are anoxic. Neutral molecular formulas provide acid-base bookkeeping; actual protonation follows the stated pH. The environment_formed field identifies the principal formation environment where multiple environments apply. Non-target terminal products use the schema's intermediate role because no byproduct role is available. Valine is racemic, competing sinks are explicit, and no cumulative yield or geochemically closed synthesis is claimed.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propan-2-one", "common_name": "acetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile", "common_name": "acetone cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H9NOS", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide", "common_name": "acetone-derived alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H10O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4,6H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H10O2", "iupac_name": "3-hydroxy-2,2-dimethylpropanal", "common_name": "hydroxypivaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2,4-dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "pantoic acid nitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H12O4", "iupac_name": "2,4-dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "pantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C6H10O3", "iupac_name": "3-hydroxy-4,4-dimethyloxolan-2-one", "common_name": "pantolactone", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "butanal", "common_name": "butyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-2-3-4-5/h4H,2-3H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "butan-2-one", "common_name": "methyl ethyl ketone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-3-4(2)5/h3H2,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-norvaline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-2-3-4(6)5(7)8/h4H,2-3,6H2,1H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-2-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-isovaline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3-5(2,6)4(7)8/h3,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous capture of cyanide and carbonyl precursors", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_017", "mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K bulk gas and aqueous trap; discharge channels are transiently much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic methane/ammonia-containing gas, electrical discharge, water-vapor circulation and prompt condensation. Repeated discharge over days is an experimental analogue, not a specified natural production rate. Carbonyl concentrations and their isomer distribution must be measured."}, "mechanism": "A coarse-grained source reaction representing radical chemistry followed by capture, not one elementary reaction. One atom-balanced bookkeeping realization is: 14 methane + ammonia + 4 water -> hydrogen cyanide + isobutyraldehyde + formaldehyde + butanal + butan-2-one + 20 hydrogen. These coefficients are neither measured yields nor predicted product ratios.", "reasoning": "Valine, norvaline and isovaline occur in reducing-gas discharge products (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009). Their assignment here to particular carbonyl precursors is a mechanistic network interpretation, not proof that this precursor mixture was isolated in those proportions. This source is conditional on a locally reducing atmosphere or plume; yields from weakly reducing mixtures can be much lower (Schlesinger and Miller, 1983). It should not be interpreted as a selective isobutyraldehyde synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K proposed surface implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline, approximately pH 8-9, with cyanide availability maintained. A 10-100 mM substrate screening range is proposed, not reported as a geochemical concentration or an optimized literature recipe. Limit volatile losses and avoid prolonged heating."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the acetone carbonyl followed by proton transfer produces the C4 cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "This entry reaction belongs to the acetone-to-valine-precursor sequence described by Patel et al. (2015). The equilibrium requires sufficient cyanide activity; neither acetone availability nor concentration of cyanide in an ordinary ocean is established. Local concentration or replenishment is necessary, consistent with the dilution problem discussed by Stribling and Miller (1987)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K proposed implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-containing solution; approximately pH 7-9 maintains some nucleophilic HS-. Use a separate sulfide-addition stage. Concentration and residence time require validation against the original experimental protocol; no conversion rate is assumed here."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the alpha-hydroxythioamide. Net: cyanohydrin + hydrogen sulfide -> hydroxythioamide.", "reasoning": "The sulfur-mediated intermediate is supported by the cyanosulfidic sequence summarized from Patel et al. (2015). Sulfide is a consumed reagent, not a mineral catalyst. Sustained dissolved sulfide is a major environmental constraint: ordinary volcanic outgassing does not necessarily provide the required HS- inventories (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Photoreductive processing to the isobutyraldehyde level", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K proposed surface implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "CuCN-derived copper-cyanide photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation near the laboratory 254 nm benchmark, staged sulfide supply and anoxic conditions. Use the demonstrated copper formulation rather than substituting an unspecified copper mineral. Numerical copper loading and irradiation dose are not supplied in the research extract and must be checked in the primary methods. Capture the carbonyl promptly; an accumulating free-aldehyde pool is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Coarse-grained photodeoxygenation, reductive thioamide processing and carbonyl release. A formal balanced closure is: 4 hydroxythioamide + 4 hydrogen sulfide -> 4 isobutyraldehyde + 4 ammonia + cyclooctasulfur. S8 represents an idealized oxidized-sulfur endpoint; the node does not assert measured sulfur speciation or a single elementary step.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) supports staged access to the valine aminonitrile through this carbonyl level, generally captured downstream as a cyanohydrin. Representing free isobutyraldehyde and S8 explicitly is a reduced-resolution mechanistic model, not a claim that either was isolated in the displayed stoichiometry. Direct diversion of the transient aldehyde into ammonia-first Strecker chemistry is an extrapolation. Sulfide availability, copper speciation, UV spectrum and operator-like staging remain unresolved environmental issues (Ranjan et al., 2018). No cumulative valine yield is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-9 implementation window. Cyanide is available before substantial ammonia addition. Prompt capture limits aldehyde volatilization and competing aldol reactions; avoid treating a highly dilute ocean as the reaction medium."}, "mechanism": "Reversible nucleophilic addition of cyanide to isobutyraldehyde gives the racemic C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin trapping is part of the demonstrated cyanosulfidic route to the valine aminonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). This branch is chemically distinguished from ammonia-first capture by reagent order, but both access an interconnected carbonyl/cyanohydrin/imine system rather than independent synthesis frameworks."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ammonia-first Strecker formation of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9, ammonia in excess over aldehyde, and replenished cyanide. Millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar aldehyde is a concentration target for testing, not an established natural inventory. If supplied from photochemistry, ammonia addition follows that stage and requires compatibility testing."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia and aldehyde establish a carbinolamine/imine equilibrium; cyanide addition to the imine or iminium species yields racemic valine aminonitrile and water.", "reasoning": "The isobutyraldehyde Strecker end-game is established, including prebiotic aerosol/cyanide-source variants discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). No mineral catalyst is required. Its principal limitations are aldehyde supply, cyanide dilution and competition from cyanohydrin or formaldehyde-dependent chemistry, not uncertainty about the basic C-N and C-C bond-forming reactions. Capture directly after rxn_004 is a conditional implementation variant rather than the specifically demonstrated staged sequence."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ammonia conversion of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia is introduced after cyanohydrin formation; proposed pH 8-9. Separate this operation from sulfide photochemistry. Maintain ammonia activity and assay residual cyanohydrin rather than assuming quantitative exchange."}, "mechanism": "Net replacement of cyanohydrin hydroxyl by amino functionality proceeds through reversible carbonyl release, imine formation and cyanide recapture; it is not asserted to be direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Conversion of the corresponding cyanohydrin-level intermediate to the valine aminonitrile by ammonia addition is supported by Patel et al. (2015). The staged sequence is stronger evidence than an assumed one-pot mixture. Its cumulative efficiency and tolerance of a natural photochemical product mixture remain unquantified in the supplied evidence."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Acid-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K proposed geothermal implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate acidic compartment, proposed pH 1-2, reached only after aminonitrile production. Days-to-weeks residence is a testing window, not a demonstrated completion time. Acidification requires management of residual volatile HCN and H2S. Thermal hydrolysis can occur in shaded subsurface portions of an otherwise UV-exposed surface system."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration to an amide and amide hydrolysis. Net neutral-form bookkeeping: valine aminonitrile + 2 water -> valine + ammonia; ammonia is predominantly ammonium at this pH.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis is the established Strecker end-game underlying the valine syntheses reviewed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). The specific dilute-acid, sub-boiling geological window here is an extrapolated implementation, not a reported optimized valine experiment. Complete hydrolysis, competing decomposition and residence time must be measured; observation of an aminonitrile alone does not establish production of free valine."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Alkaline hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide-rich aqueous medium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K proposed implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Base-promoted nitrile and amide hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 11-12 in a concentrated alkaline geothermal pool, after aminonitrile formation. Sustained alkalinity is required; the immediate product is valinate. Days-to-months is a screening window, not a validated rate. Account for amide accumulation, cyanide loss and degradation."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the nitrile gives an amide-level intermediate; further hydrolysis yields valinate and ammonia. Proton equilibration gives the neutral formula represented by mol_014. Net neutral-form bookkeeping uses two water molecules.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted nitrile hydrolysis is established chemistry, but this valine-specific prebiotic operating window is speculative and is not demonstrated in the supplied studies. It is included as an explicitly conditional alternative to acidic Strecker work-up, not as another proven origin of the branched skeleton. The supplied Strecker review supports the general aminonitrile end-game, not the proposed kinetics (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni supported on silica-alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K, reported experimental benchmark", "pressure": "5 bar hydrogen", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. Physical setting: cool, closed, hydrogen-pressurized aqueous rock pore or experimental analogue; no enzymes are invoked. Keep cyanide and sulfide streams separate because they can poison or alter the catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Reversible ammonia addition and imine formation are followed by Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation. Net: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + ammonia + hydrogen -> valine + water.", "reasoning": "Valine formation from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024), including the absence of reduction without a metal catalyst. Supported Ni is the demonstrated catalyst; replacing it with an unspecified natural mineral is not assumed equivalent. Exposed native Ni on silicate could be an analogue, but its local loading and persistence require evidence. The unresolved ketoacid supply remains a boundary assumption, and carbonyl reduction competes through rxn_013."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Palladium on carbon"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K, reported experimental benchmark", "pressure": "5 bar hydrogen", "catalyst": "Pd/C, 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. A cool pressurized aqueous compartment is required. This is a laboratory-supported catalyst benchmark, not an assertion that naturally occurring Pd/C deposits were available."}, "mechanism": "Palladium activates hydrogen and reduces the ketoacid-derived imine to racemic valine, releasing water in the overall reaction.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly supports this final transformation and reports improved amino-acid versus hydroxy-acid selectivity with Pd across the tested substrate set. A valine-specific numerical yield is not assigned from pooled ranges. Palladium rarity, its support and reactive surface availability make this a geochemically conditional route despite strong reaction-level evidence; it does not solve alpha-ketoisovalerate supply."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite with exposed nickel-containing metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K, reported experimental benchmark", "pressure": "5 bar hydrogen", "catalyst": "Ground Aletai meteorite, reported loading approximately 18 mol% Ni", "aqueous": true, "other": "30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate, pH 8, 72 h. The reported experiment used 3 mg meteorite; scaling must preserve metal-to-substrate loading. Particle size, aliquot volume and pretreatment must be obtained from the primary methods rather than inferred from meteorite mass alone. A natural analogue requires freshly exposed metal and sustained hydrogen."}, "mechanism": "Reactive metallic sites activate hydrogen and hydrogenate the ammonia-derived imine; competing ketoacid hydrogenation produces hydroxyisovalerate.", "reasoning": "Ground iron-meteorite catalysis of the valine end-game is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024). Meteorite occurrence alone does not guarantee equivalent surface area, exposed Ni, corrosion state or catalytic lifetime. This route retains both the purchased-substrate limitation and the competing reduction sink, without invoking enzymes or an unverified upstream carboxylation."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to hydroxyisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or exposed Aletai Fe-Ni metal, used as alternative catalysts"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar hydrogen", "catalyst": "The Ni or meteorite catalyst specified in rxn_010 or rxn_012", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same pH 8, 30 mM ketoacid, ammonium-containing medium and 72 h benchmark as the corresponding amination branch. This is a competing reaction in those mixtures, not a claim that both alternative catalysts must be combined."}, "mechanism": "Direct hydrogenation of the keto carbonyl, rather than its ammonia-derived imine, yields racemic alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reports substantial hydroxy-acid competition with Ni and meteorite catalysts. The network therefore does not treat ketoacid consumption as synonymous with valine yield or assign all hydrogenation flux to amination. Exact valine-specific branching fractions are not supplied by the research summary."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Formaldehyde-dependent diversion of isobutyraldehyde by aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous inorganic phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K proposed implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Phosphate-mediated general acid-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral pH, approximately 6.5-7.5, with both aldehydes and dissolved phosphate present. Reactant concentrations, phosphate loading and residence time should follow the primary aldol experiments before quantitative competition with Strecker chemistry is inferred."}, "mechanism": "Enolization of isobutyraldehyde followed by addition to formaldehyde produces hydroxypivaldehyde, consuming the branched aldehyde otherwise available for valine synthesis.", "reasoning": "The neutral-pH, phosphate-catalyzed aldol diversion is supported by Fairchild et al. (2024). Phosphate is included for a specific catalytic function, not merely because it is a common prebiotic mineral constituent. Its effect on valine yield depends on formaldehyde concentration and reaction timing; complete diversion is not assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation on hydroxypivaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-9 with cyanide available and ammonia low enough that the cyanohydrin branch is competitive. This operation follows aldol addition; the branch fraction is not specified."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the remaining aldehyde carbonyl, forming the nitrile precursor of pantoic acid.", "reasoning": "Pantoic-acid production from isobutyraldehyde, formaldehyde and cyanide is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993), while Fairchild et al. (2024) supports the upstream aldol chemistry. This node is a chemically resolved representation of that diversion, not a measured intermediate yield under the proposed environmental window."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Hydrolysis of pantoic acid nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K proposed geothermal implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate acidic stage, proposed pH 1-2. Monitor nitrile and amide disappearance over days to weeks; complete conversion at these conditions is not established by the supplied extract. Residual cyanide must not be assumed compatible with the acidification stage."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis gives pantoic acid and ammonia. Net: pantoic acid nitrile + 2 water -> pantoic acid + ammonia.", "reasoning": "The overall pantoic-acid branch is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993). The proposed geological hydrolysis window is a conditional implementation of established nitrile chemistry, not a validated residence-time prediction. Partial hydrolysis can retain material in nitrile or amide pools, so pantolactone formation is not assumed quantitative."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Pantoic acid lactonization", "inputs": ["mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K proposed implementation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic concentrating films or pools, approximately pH 2-4 while wet, followed by partial drying. Reduced water activity favors lactonization; rewetting permits reverse hydrolysis. No fixed conversion or residence time is inferred."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular esterification between the carboxyl group and terminal hydroxyl group forms the five-membered pantolactone ring and releases water.", "reasoning": "This closes the pantoate/pantolactone sink associated with isobutyraldehyde chemistry (Miller and Schlesinger, 1993; Fairchild et al., 2024). Acid catalysis and water activity are specified because efficient cyclization cannot be assumed in unspecified neutral bulk water. The detailed wet-dry implementation remains an environmental extrapolation."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Competing butanal Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis to norvaline", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity during the hydrolysis stage only"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K for Strecker capture, then 353-370 K for proposed hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "None for Strecker capture; acid catalysis for subsequent hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Sequential ammonia/cyanide-containing medium near pH 8-9, then a separate acidic hydrolysis stage near pH 1-2. Hydrolysis completion at the proposed geothermal conditions must be measured. Resolve the final C5 amino-acid isomers chromatographically."}, "mechanism": "Coarse-grained imine formation, cyanide addition and aminonitrile hydrolysis. Net: butanal + hydrogen cyanide + ammonia + water -> norvaline + ammonia. Ammonia occurs on both sides because its nitrogen is incorporated while nitrile-derived nitrogen is released; the process is not ammonia-free.", "reasoning": "Norvaline is a documented coproduct of valine-producing discharge experiments (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009), and butanal gives its expected Strecker carbon skeleton. This net branch tracks chemical and analytical competition without asserting a measured norvaline/valine ratio or identical rates for the isomeric substrates."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Competing butan-2-one Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis to isovaline", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_004", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous proton activity during the hydrolysis stage only"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K for Strecker capture, then 353-370 K for proposed hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "None for Strecker capture; acid catalysis for subsequent hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia/cyanide-containing medium near pH 8-9 followed by separate acidic hydrolysis near pH 1-2. Ketone-derived aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis need not match the aldehyde-derived rates. Confirm isovaline separately from valine and norvaline."}, "mechanism": "Ketimine formation and cyanide addition establish the alpha-methyl aminonitrile skeleton; hydrolysis yields racemic isovaline. Net: butan-2-one + hydrogen cyanide + ammonia + water -> isovaline + ammonia.", "reasoning": "Isovaline accompanies valine in discharge products (Ring et al., 1972; Johnson et al., 2009), and its relationship to butan-2-one is consistent with Strecker interpretation of meteoritic amino-acid mixtures. This is a competing isomer branch, not evidence for selective valine production. The exact geological hydrolysis schedule remains conditional."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Boundary aldehyde, ammonia-first Strecker, acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Externally supplied isobutyraldehyde enters the established Strecker end-game. Aldehyde availability is a boundary assumption; the proposed geothermal hydrolysis rate is not established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Boundary aldehyde, cyanohydrin-first capture, acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Isobutyraldehyde is captured with cyanide before ammonia addition. This reagent-order variant shares the aminonitrile endpoint with P1 and is not an independent carbon-skeleton origin.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Acetone homologation with ammonia-first interception", "description": "The supported acetone homologation chemistry supplies a transient aldehyde-level intermediate, followed by direct Strecker interception. Interception before cyanohydrin accumulation is a conditional staging extrapolation, not the specific demonstrated Patel sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Staged cyanosulfidic acetone-to-valine-precursor route", "description": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation, sulfur-mediated photoreductive homologation, cyanohydrin capture and ammonia addition follow the strongest supplied evidence for assembling the branched precursor. Hydrolysis extends the demonstrated aminonitrile endpoint to free racemic valine; cumulative yield and natural staging remain unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Discharge-fed ammonia-first Strecker route", "description": "Reducing-gas discharge supplies a modeled mixture of carbonyls and cyanide; isobutyraldehyde undergoes Strecker capture and acidic hydrolysis. Valine occurrence in discharge experiments is established, but this resolved precursor sequence and its flux are inferred.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Discharge-fed cyanohydrin-first Strecker route", "description": "The same conditional discharge source feeds cyanohydrin capture before