{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:00:15.826891", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Valine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Valine: A Cross-Framework Evidence Review\n\nValine is one of the most robustly \"prebiotically accessible\" proteinogenic amino acids at the level of detection \u2014 it appears in spark-discharge products, carbonaceous chondrites, asteroid-return samples and interstellar-ice photolysis residues \u2014 but no single route yet delivers it selectively, at high yield, from simple feedstocks under one set of geochemically defended conditions. The best-characterized end-game steps are (i) Strecker chemistry on isobutyraldehyde and (ii) metal-catalyzed reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (\u03b1-KIV); the critical bottleneck in every framework is the prebiotic origin of the branched C4/C5 skeleton (isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV), and the biosynthetic pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV sequence has no published nonenzymatic demonstration.\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- **Valine forms easily but non-selectively.** It has been confirmed in spark-discharge products (Ring et al. 1972, PNAS; Johnson et al. 2009, OLEB reanalysis of Miller's original samples), in Murchison and other CM chondrites, in Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, Nature Astronomy), and in UV-irradiated interstellar ice analogues (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, Nature) \u2014 always as a minor, racemic component accompanied by its isomers norvaline and isovaline.\n- **Two end-game chemistries are experimentally solid; the skeleton is not.** Isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (Strecker) and \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni, Pd or ground iron meteorite (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem; 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, no reaction without catalyst) both give valine, but how isobutyraldehyde or \u03b1-KIV arise prebiotically is either demonstrated only in a multistep, operator-staged network (Patel et al. 2015, Nature Chemistry: acetone \u2192 valine aminonitrile via HCN/H2S/UV/Cu) or not demonstrated at all (nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway).\n- **All routes are contested.** Spark discharge depends on reducing atmospheres; cyanosulfidic chemistry on high sulfide, 254-nm UV and staged reagent delivery; metabolism-first routes on a supply of \u03b1-KIV that nobody has made abiotically; and extraterrestrial delivery supplies only nmol/g-level valine. For network design, treat valine as a downstream \"skeleton-limited\" target and prioritize finding a prebiotic source of the C5 branched skeleton.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n| Route / framework | Feedstocks | Key valine-relevant step | Valine actually detected/produced? | Catalyst | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark discharge (Miller-Urey and variants) | CH4, N2/NH3, H2O, H2 (also CO/CO2 + H2) | Radical gas-phase chemistry \u2192 aldehydes + HCN \u2192 Strecker in aqueous phase | Yes (Ring et al. 1972; Johnson et al. 2009 reanalysis) | None required | Contested (atmospheric redox state) |\n| Strecker synthesis on isobutyraldehyde | Isobutyraldehyde, HCN, NH3 | \u03b1-aminonitrile \u2192 hydrolysis | Yes (textbook; prebiotic variants incl. ferrocyanide/aerosol, Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013) | None required | End-game solid; aldehyde origin contested |\n| Cyanosulfidic network (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, phosphate, Cu, 254-nm UV | Acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation \u2192 valine \u03b1-aminonitrile | Valine precursor (aminonitrile) shown; no yield stated | Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox, HS\u207b reductant | Contested (sulfide levels, UV, staging) |\n| Reductive amination of \u03b1-KIV | \u03b1-KIV, NH3/NH4\u207a, H2 (or formate) | Imine formation + hydrogenation | Yes (Kaur et al. 2024, Chem) | Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, Pd/C, Aletai iron meteorite; none = no reaction | End-game solid; \u03b1-KIV origin unsolved |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs on \u03b1-ketoacids | \u03b1-ketoacid, HCN, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin \u2192 hydrolysis | General for \u03b1-ketoacids (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, Nature Chemistry) | None (bicarbonate buffer) | Requires \u03b1-KIV; cyanide relevance debated |\n| Biosynthetic analog (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 DHIV \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) | Pyruvate | ThDP condensation, reductoisomerization, dehydration | **No nonenzymatic demonstration** | \u2014 | Hypothetical |\n| Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) | CO, H2, NH3 | Surface catalysis at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C | \"Several \u03b1-amino acids\" (Yoshino et al. 1971); not reproduced robustly | Fe\u2013Ni metal, magnetite | Contested |\n| HCN polymer hydrolysis | HCN | Oligomer hydrolysis | Not in standard HCN oligomers (Ferris et al. 1974); reported after \u2076\u2070Co \u03b3-irradiation of HCN | None | Weak for valine |\n| Interstellar ice UV photolysis | H2O, CH3OH, NH3, CO, CO2 (\u00b1HCN) | Radical chemistry at 12 K, hydrolysis of residue | Yes in Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; ee measured by Modica et al. 2014 | None (UV) | Accepted as possible source of meteoritic organics |\n| Impact shock | Comet-ice analogue | Shock heating | Not reported (glycine, alanine, AIB, isovaline only; Martins et al. 2013) | None | Not shown for valine |\n| Meteoritic/asteroidal delivery | Parent-body aqueous chemistry | Strecker/cyanohydrin in parent body | Yes, nmol/g level; racemic or contested L-excess | Parent-body minerals | Occurrence accepted; contribution debated |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Spark-discharge / Miller-Urey-type experiments\n\n**What was shown.** Ring, Wolman, Friedmann and Miller (1972, *PNAS* 69:765) ran electric discharges on CH4 + N2 + H2O with traces of NH3 and confirmed glycine, alanine, \u03b1-amino-n-butyric acid, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid, **valine, norvaline, isovaline**, leucine, isoleucine, alloisoleucine, norleucine, proline, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, serine, threonine and others.\\[1\\]\\[2\\] This is the classic demonstration that the hydrophobic branched amino acids form alongside their unbranched isomers \u2014 valine is produced together with norvaline and isovaline, so any analysis must resolve the C5 isomers chromatographically.\n\n**Archived-sample reanalyses.** After Stanley Miller's death in 2007, Bada and colleagues found his dried 1950s residues. Johnson et al. (2008, *Science* 322:404) showed that the \"volcanic\" apparatus (an aspirator injecting steam into the spark flask) gave 22 amino acids and five amines, more diverse than the classic apparatus. Johnson et al. (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 39:240) reported that the original samples contain several aminobutyric acid isomers plus serine, homoserine, isoserine, isovaline, **valine**, phenylalanine, ornithine and others, and that both diversity and yield increased with the water-aspirating device. Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) analyzed an unpublished 1958 H2S-rich experiment (CH4, NH3, H2S, CO2), reporting the first sulfur amino acids from spark experiments and the highest relative yields of aminobutyric acid isomers ever found in a spark experiment; that paper's emphasis is on sulfur and C4 amino acids, and this review does not rely on it for a valine-specific yield.\\[3\\]\n\n**Conditions and yields.** Ambient temperature, 1 bar-scale gas mixtures, electrical energy; no catalyst. Schlesinger and Miller (1983, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) showed that total amino acid yields are 1.2\u20134.7% (based on carbon) for CH4-containing mixtures largely independent of H2/CH4 ratio, about 2% for H2 + CO or H2 + CO2 mixtures at H2/C ratios of 2\u20134, but only ~10\u207b\u00b3% when H2/CO2 = 0.\\[4\\] Higher amino acids such as valine are a small fraction of the total.\n\n**Mechanism.** Discharge produces HCN, aldehydes and ketones in the gas phase; amino acids form in the aqueous trap by Strecker (aldehyde + HCN + NH3) and related cyanohydrin chemistry. Valine thus derives from isobutyraldehyde formed by gas-phase radical recombination.\n\n**Critiques.** The main objection is atmospheric redox state: if the Hadean atmosphere was CO2/N2-dominated, yields collapse (the 10\u207b\u00b3% figure above). Defenders argue that volcanic plumes (Johnson et al. 2008) or transiently H2-rich post-impact atmospheres provide local reducing conditions. A second critique is selectivity: valine is a minor product in a broad mixture with non-proteinogenic isomers.\n\n### 2. Strecker synthesis from isobutyraldehyde \u2014 and the origin of isobutyraldehyde\n\n**Chemistry.** Isobutyraldehyde (2-methylpropanal) + NH3 + HCN \u2192 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (valine \u03b1-aminonitrile) \u2192 hydrolysis \u2192 racemic valine. No catalyst is required; the reaction proceeds in water at ambient temperature, with the aminonitrile/cyanohydrin partition controlled by NH3/HCN ratios and pH. Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) used aqueous aerosols with UV and ferrocyanide or Prussian Blue as the cyanide source to perform Strecker syntheses of alanine, valine and phenylalanine, presenting ferrocyanides as a way to store cyanide in an Fe(II)-bearing ocean.\n\n**Competing sinks for isobutyraldehyde.** Isobutyraldehyde is also the precursor of pantoic acid: Miller and Schlesinger (1993, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) reported that pantoic acid forms in good prebiotic yield from isobutyraldehyde (or \u03b1-KIV) + H2CO + HCN. Fairchild et al. (2024, *Science*; Powner group) showed a phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol of formaldehyde with isobutyraldehyde \u2014 the Strecker precursors of glycine and valine \u2014 selectively leading to pantolactone/pantetheine. This shows that, in a mixture rich in formaldehyde, isobutyraldehyde is partly diverted from valine toward coenzyme A precursors; network designers should model this branch explicitly.\n\n**Critiques.** Strecker chemistry itself is uncontroversial; the open question is the supply of isobutyraldehyde. Proposed sources include spark/plume chemistry (above), the cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone (below), FTT chemistry, and decarboxylation of \u03b1-KIV (which presupposes \u03b1-KIV). Dilute HCN is a further problem: Stribling and Miller (1987, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere* 17:261) calculated, for an HCN production rate of 100 nmol cm\u207b\u00b2 yr\u207b\u00b9, a steady-state primitive-ocean HCN concentration of about 4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C, pushing Strecker chemistry toward evaporitic, eutectic-freezing or ferrocyanide-storage scenarios.\n\n### 3. Cyanosulfidic / systems-chemistry route (Sutherland group)\n\n**What was shown.** Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry* 7:301) derived precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids by reductive homologation of HCN and its derivatives. Valine is accessible: dihydroxyacetone reduction gives acetone; acetone + HCN gives a cyanohydrin; reaction with H2S gives an \u03b1-hydroxythioamide; Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycling under 254-nm UV with HS\u207b as stoichiometric reductant removes the hydroxyl to give the isobutyraldehyde-level product, which is trapped as a cyanohydrin and converted by NH3 to the **valine \u03b1-aminonitrile**. The authors demonstrated the conversion of the constitutionally related intermediate to the valine aminonitrile by addition of ammonia and noted that a further homologation cycle gives the leucine aminonitrile. The network accesses 12 amino acids via Strecker intermediates. A later paper (Liu et al. 2023, *Nature Chemistry*, \"Thiophosphate photochemistry enables prebiotic access to sugars and terpenoid precursors\") showed thiophosphate (PSO3\u00b3\u207b) photochemistry improves parts of this network and preserves access to valine and leucine.\n\n**Yields.** Patel et al. explicitly decline to give discrete yields for individual products, arguing that in a multi-product network the yield of any one compound is less meaningful; no percentage is published for the valine aminonitrile from acetone. Benchmark homologation steps in related Sutherland-group work (e.g., glycolaldehyde ~42% after 4 h with CuCN/H2S under UV) indicate individual steps can be efficient, but the cumulative yield of the multistep valine sequence is unknown.\n\n**Environment proposed.** Surface streams and pools on an early Earth with UV exposure, cyanide stored as ferrocyanide salts (released by impact/thermal processing), sulfide from volcanism or impacts, and \"confluence\" of streams delivering different intermediates.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Sulfide availability: Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland and Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology* 18:1023) concluded that volcanic outgassing \"could have supplied prebiotically relevant levels of SO2-derived anions, but not H2S-derived anions,\" and that \"epochs of moderately high volcanism could have been especially conducive to cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry,\" so the chemistry needs specific local settings (e.g., impact-supplied sulfide or high-outgassing epochs). (ii) The use of 254-nm UV and Cu catalysts, and stepwise reagent addition by the experimenter, is criticized as idealized relative to a natural setting. (iii) Congruence with biochemistry: the route reaches valine via aminonitriles, not via \u03b1-KIV and transamination as in extant metabolism. (iv) Comment literature notes the chemistry requires H2S from the outset, possibly corresponding to a later geochemical stage.\n\n### 4. Reductive amination and transamination of \u03b1-ketoisovalerate (metal/mineral catalysis)\n\n**Strongest valine-specific demonstration.** Kaur, Rauscher, Werner, Song, Yi, Kaz\u00f6ne, Martin, T\u00fcys\u00fcz and Moran (2024, *Chem* 10:1) showed that H2 drives nonenzymatic reductive amination of six biological \u03b1-ketoacids \u2014 pyruvate, \u03b1-ketoglutarate, oxaloacetate, \u03b1-ketoisocaproate, **\u03b1-ketoisovalerate**, \u03b1-keto-\u03b2-methylvalerate \u2014 and glyoxylate to glycine, alanine, glutamate, aspartate, leucine, **valine** and isoleucine.\\[5\\] Conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, 5 bar H2, NH4HCO3 (5 equiv, 150 mM), pH 8 water, 22 \u00b0C, 72 h, with Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 (10 mol% Ni), Pd/C (1 mol%) or 3 mg ground Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni). With Ni and meteorite, combined amino + hydroxy acid yields were 6\u201384% at roughly 1:1 amino:hydroxy acid; with Pd, 52\u201388% with >5:1 selectivity for the amino acid in almost all cases. No reduction occurred without a metal catalyst. For pyruvate, ammonia as low as 6 mM gave alanine in up to 17% (Ni) or 44% (Pd) yield relative to ammonia \u2014 62 to 250 times lower ammonia than earlier prebiotic reductive aminations (375 mM\u20131.5 M). The same conditions run H2-driven reductive aldol chemistry that builds \u03b1-ketoglutarate-family ketoacids, but not the branched \u03b1-KIV skeleton. The authors cite 1\u201315 mmol/kg dissolved H2 in hydrothermal fluids as a geological analog to their H2 pressures.\n\n**Earlier iron-sulfide and iron-hydroxide work.** Hafenbradl, Keller, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and Stetter (1995, *Tetrahedron Letters*) coupled reductive amination of \u03b1-oxo acids to oxidative pyrite formation (FeS/H2S).\\[6\\]\\[7\\] Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695) reported efficient reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 at pH near the amine pKa, using stoichiometric Fe(II) reductant and high ammonia.\\[8\\] Barge, Flores, Baum, VanderVelde and Russell (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828) showed mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides (\"green rust\") convert pyruvate to alanine and lactate; alanine is favored at intermediate Fe redox state (~50% Fe(II)), at pH above the NH3 pKa (~9.25), and at 25\u201370 \u00b0C, with no alanine at pH 7.\\[9\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances*) electroreduced metal sulfides (e.g., FeS \u2192 Fe\u2070) at potentials expected at alkaline vent\u2013seawater interfaces and achieved >50% yields for reductive amination of four keto acids (1.25 mM each) in one pot. These studies did not focus on \u03b1-KIV; valine is expected by analogy but was not the reported target in the iron-mineral papers cited here.\n\n**Reactivity data.** Mayer and Moran (2022, *Angewandte Chemie*) quantified nonenzymatic reduction and reductive amination of keto acids with BH3CN\u207b as a model hydride, showing \u03b1-ketoglutarate is the least reactive Krebs ketoacid toward amination\\[12\\] \u2014 relevant for predicting partitioning between \u03b1-KIV and other ketoacids in mixtures.\n\n**Critiques.** (i) Substrate supply: every reductive-amination demonstration starts from purchased \u03b1-KIV; no abiotic \u03b1-KIV synthesis exists (see \u00a75). (ii) Competing carbonyl reduction yields \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate in roughly equal amounts with Ni/meteorite catalysts. (iii) Earlier Fe(II)-mediated work required 375 mM\u20131.5 M NH3, considered implausibly high; Kaur et al. reduce this but require concentrated metal catalysts (or Pd, which is rare). (iv) Metal-catalyzed hydrogenation is a textbook industrial reaction, and critics question whether native Ni or platinum-group metals were locally concentrated; proponents point to awaruite (Ni3Fe) in serpentinites, metallic Ni particles in H2-rich vents and ~220 tonnes/year of Fe-Ni cosmic dust.\n\n### 5. Metabolism-first: the biosynthetic analogy (pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate \u2192 \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine)\n\nIn biology, acetohydroxyacid synthase (ThDP-dependent) condenses two pyruvates to (S)-2-acetolactate + CO2; ketol-acid reductoisomerase (Mg\u00b2\u207a/NADPH) performs an alkyl migration plus reduction to 2,3-dihydroxyisovalerate; dihydroxyacid dehydratase ([Fe\u2013S] enzyme) gives \u03b1-KIV; and transamination yields valine.\\[13\\]\\[14\\]\n\n**Evidence status.** A targeted literature search found **no peer-reviewed nonenzymatic demonstration** of any of the three skeleton-building steps. Thiazolium salts catalyze benzoin/acyloin condensations of aldehydes (Sheehan and Hara 1974, *Journal of Organic Chemistry*), but no prebiotic, metal- or mineral-catalyzed pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate conversion has been reported. Acetolactate is known to undergo nonenzymatic oxidative decarboxylation to diacetyl/acetoin, i.e., abiotic chemistry tends to degrade rather than build this intermediate. The reductoisomerization couples methyl migration and reduction in a single active site, with intermediates not released, which makes a nonenzymatic analog mechanistically demanding.\n\n**Iron-promoted networks.** Muchowska, Varma and Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104) showed Fe\u00b2\u207a in 70 \u00b0C water drives a pyruvate + glyoxylate network that reproduces 9 of 11 Krebs-cycle intermediates and all five universal metabolic precursors, and with hydroxylamine and metallic iron gives four amino acids (glycine, alanine, aspartate, glutamate); valine is not among them. Stubbs et al. (2020, *Nature Chemistry*) and Krishnamurthy-group cyanide-reductant chemistry (2022, *Nature Chemistry*) build \u03b1-ketoacid networks from glyoxylate and pyruvate, also without the branched-chain pathway.\n\n**Alternative evolutionary hypothesis.** Keefe, Lazcano and Miller (1995, *Origins of Life and Evolution of the Biosphere*) argued the acetolactate pathway cannot be the primordial route because it has unstable intermediates, and proposed that branched-chain amino acids were first made by reductive carboxylation of short branched-chain fatty acids (isobutyric acid \u2192 \u03b1-KIV) followed by transamination. This is a proposal, not an experimental demonstration.\n\n**Thermodynamics.** Amend and Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659) computed the net synthesis 5CO2(aq) + NH4\u207a + 12H2(aq) \u2192 valine(aq) + H\u207a + 8H2O. They report that \"\u0394Gr is negative for 11 of the 20 net amino acid synthesis reactions in a 100\u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution,\" whereas \"all 20 amino acids are strongly endergonic in cooler (18\u00b0C), oxidized, surface seawater\"; the paper's valine table row (entries of \u221270.12 and 178.00) was not verified here as to which quantity each represents, so per-valine kJ/mol values should be taken from the primary table. This establishes thermodynamic drive in H2-rich settings but says nothing about kinetic pathways; Aubrey, Cleaves and Bada (2009, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) cautioned that such equilibrium models neglect kinetics and degradation, observing A \u21cc B \u21cc C (precursor \u21cc amino acid \u21cc degradation product) behavior at 125\u2013175 \u00b0C.\n\n**Critiques.** The framework predicts congruence with extant biochemistry (\u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine by amination), which the Kaur et al. data support for the final step, but the absence of a nonenzymatic \u03b1-KIV synthesis is the single largest gap in the metabolism-first account of valine. High temperatures also accelerate amino acid decomposition.\n\n### 6. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry on \u03b1-ketoacids\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen and Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142) showed \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia in bicarbonate buffer at room temperature via the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway to hydantoins, which hydrolyze (80 \u00b0C) to \u03b1-amino acids; e.g., \u03b1-ketoglutarate gave the hydantoin in 96% yield, with slower hydrolysis to glutamate (19% with 72% hydantoin remaining). The route bridges cyanide chemistry and ketoacid metabolism. For valine it would require \u03b1-KIV and so inherits the same skeleton problem. Kaur et al. (2024) criticize cyanide-based routes as incongruent with metabolism, since cyanide is never directly used in biochemistry and is toxic to metalloenzymes.\n\n### 7. Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type (FTT) and CO/H2 surface chemistry\n\nYoshino, Hayatsu and Anders (1971, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 35:927) reported several \u03b1-amino acids and \u03b2-alanine from CO + H2 + NH3 over Fe\u2013Ni catalysts at 200\u2013500 \u00b0C, proposed as a solar-nebula source of meteoritic organics. A NASA follow-up (NASA TM X-62,411, 1975) heating CO/H2/NH3 with iron meteorite found only small quantities of a few amino acids, \"at odds with the existing literature.\" Glavin et al. (2010, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) and Elsila et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) note that FTT synthesis needs re-examination with modern analytics. Valine specifically has not been robustly shown by FTT; the route is contested.\n\n### 8. HCN polymer hydrolysis, formamide and radiolysis\n\nFerris, Wos, Nooner and Or\u00f3 (1974, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 3:225) identified glycine, aspartic acid and diaminosuccinic acid as major and alanine, isoleucine and \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid as minor amino acids from hydrolyzed HCN oligomers \u2014 valine is not among them. Ferris et al. (1978, *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 11:293) showed 0.1 M HCN oligomerizes at pH 9.2. Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life* 7:187) exposed aqueous HCN solutions (0.004\u20130.1 M) to cobalt-60 gamma rays and identified glycine, alanine, valine, serine, threonine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid among the hydrolysis products; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013, *Life*) list 12 of 20 protein amino acids across HCN-polymer studies. Valine is therefore a minor, condition-dependent product of HCN polymer chemistry rather than a characteristic one. Formamide-based chemistry has been developed mainly for nucleobases; this review found no valine-specific formamide synthesis.\n\n### 9. Interstellar ice irradiation\n\nMu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:403) UV-irradiated H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 (2:1:1:1:1) ice at 12 K and identified 16 amino acids in the hydrolyzed room-temperature residue, valine among them. Bernstein et al. (2002, *Nature* 416:401) independently found glycine, alanine and serine from H2O/CH3OH/NH3/HCN ices. Modica et al. (2014, *Astrophysical Journal*) measured small enantiomeric excesses induced by circularly polarized UV for five amino acids including valine and norvaline. Critique: products are detected only after acid hydrolysis, so precursors rather than free valine form in the ice; yields are tiny; relevance depends on delivery and survival.\n\n### 10. Impact/shock synthesis\n\nMartins, Price, Goldman, Sephton and Burchell (2013, *Nature Geoscience* 6:1045) fired projectiles at ~7 km/s into comet-ice analogues and obtained racemic alanine, \u03b1-aminoisobutyric acid and isovaline (plus precursors) after hydrolysis. Valine was not reported. Impact routes are therefore unproven for valine.\n\n### 11. Meteoritic occurrence and extraterrestrial delivery\n\n**Abundance.** Valine is a minor meteoritic amino acid. Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) reported amino acids in Aguas Zarcas and Murchison at ~0.1 to 158 nmol/g overall; valine was at the low end, too low for compound-specific \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C measurement. Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199) contain 33 amino acids including 14 of the 20 protein amino acids, valine among them, with chiral non-protein amino acids racemic or nearly so; the same paper measured an L-valine excess of ~34% in an acid-hydrolyzed hot-water extract but also observed elevated L-valine in the procedural blank, so laboratory contamination is a possible explanation.\n\n**Enantiomeric data \u2014 contested.** Murchison's D/L valine ratio of 0.67 \u00b1 0.03 (\u224820% L-excess) in Glavin et al. (2021) differs from Aguas Zarcas and Murchison soil and may be extraterrestrial, but could not be confirmed isotopically. Pizzarello and Cronin (2000, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) found \u03b1-H amino acids including valine racemic or attributed excesses to contamination, with the documented meteoritic excesses confined to \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline (up to 18.5% L in Murchison; Glavin and Dworkin 2009, *PNAS*). Dworkin et al. (2026, arXiv:2609.12249), an OSIRIS-REx contamination-knowledge paper, report that \"the hummocky sample shows an excess of ~20% L-valine (Kim et al. 2026),\" conclude that this excess \"is not attributable to spacecraft contamination,\" and cite only a modest 2.2% L-valine excess as the sole earlier chondrite report (Pizzarello and Cronin 2000). These literatures conflict; any valine L-excess in meteorites should be regarded as unconfirmed. In the primitive CM Asuka 12236, valine D/L ratios of ~0.82\u20130.89 indicate near-racemic composition (Glavin et al. 2020, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*).\n\n**Isomer context.** Meteorites contain valine together with norvaline, isovaline and other C5 amino acids, consistent with Strecker/cyanohydrin chemistry on parent bodies acting on mixtures of C4 aldehydes and ketones (isobutyraldehyde \u2192 valine; butanal \u2192 norvaline; 2-butanone \u2192 isovaline). The dominance of \u03b1-methyl amino acids such as isovaline in some chondrites contrasts with biology's selection of valine.\n\n**Critiques.** Delivery fluxes and survival through atmospheric entry/impact limit concentrations; nmol/g valine in meteorites cannot by itself sustain concentration-dependent chemistry without strong local accumulation.\n\n## Environmental Context\n\n- **Reducing atmospheres/volcanic plumes with lightning**: supported for valine by spark experiments, contingent on local reducing gas compositions (Johnson et al. 2008).\n- **UV-lit surface pools/streams with HCN, sulfide, Cu and phosphate (cyanosulfidic)**: valine aminonitrile demonstrated; sulfide and UV requirements are the main plausibility constraint, with Ranjan et al. (2018, *Astrobiology*) finding outgassing could supply SO2-derived but not H2S-derived anions at relevant levels.\n- **H2-rich hydrothermal/serpentinizing systems with Ni/Fe-Ni alloys or iron oxyhydroxides**: best supported for the final \u03b1-KIV \u2192 valine step (Kaur et al. 2024; Barge et al. 2019 for analogous pyruvate chemistry) and thermodynamically favorable (Amend and Shock 1998), but with no demonstrated \u03b1-KIV source.\n- **Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices**: proven valine sources at trace levels.\n\nOn the evidence, no single environment is demonstrated to make valine from simple feedstocks selectively. The most experimentally complete feedstock-to-valine chain is the cyanosulfidic acetone route (multistep, staged), while the most mild, catalytic and biochemically congruent final step is metal-catalyzed reductive amination.\n\n## Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **The branched-skeleton problem.** No abiotic synthesis of \u03b1-KIV is published; isobutyraldehyde arises only from non-selective discharge/FTT chemistry or the staged cyanosulfidic homologation of acetone. This is the central bottleneck for valine.\n2. **Nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway.** Pyruvate \u2192 acetolactate, reductoisomerization and dehydration all lack nonenzymatic demonstrations; finding thiazolium-free, metal-promoted pyruvate homocoupling would be a high-value experiment.\n3. **Selectivity versus isomers.** Valine co-forms with norvaline and isovaline in all non-enzymatic chemistries; mechanisms that favor the branched \u03b1-H isomer are unknown.\n4. **Concentration and compatibility.** HCN dilution (~4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 M at pH 8 and 0 \u00b0C in Stribling and Miller's 1987 primitive-ocean estimate), the ammonia requirement for amination, sulfide levels for photoredox chemistry, and isobutyraldehyde diversion to pantoate/pantetheine are all unresolved compatibility issues.\n5. **Congruence with biochemistry.** Strecker and cyanosulfidic routes do not pass through \u03b1-KIV, while metabolism-first routes match biology's final step but not its skeleton-building steps.\n6. **Chirality.** Abiotic valine is racemic; claimed meteoritic and Bennu L-excesses are contradictory and unconfirmed.\n\n## Recommendations for Synthesis-Network Design\n\n- Model two independent entry points into valine: (a) isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 (catalyst-free Strecker), and (b) \u03b1-KIV + NH3 + H2 over Ni/Fe-Ni (or formate over Pd). Treat both as demonstrated end-games.\n- Treat the upstream skeleton step as the explicit unknown: include the cyanosulfidic acetone \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 reductive homologation branch (with no published yield) and flag the biosynthetic acetolactate branch as hypothetical.\n- Include the formaldehyde + isobutyraldehyde \u2192 pantolactone side reaction and \u03b1-hydroxyisovalerate formation as competing sinks.\n- Track norvaline and isovaline as co-products and use chromatographic separation of C5 isomers in any experimental validation.\n- Prioritize experiments on nonenzymatic pyruvate homocoupling or reductive carboxylation of isobutyrate (Keefe et al. 1995 hypothesis) over further optimization of the final amination step.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Several valine-specific numbers (e.g., per-compound yields in Kaur et al. 2024 and absolute valine yields in spark experiments) are reported here only as ranges or qualitative detections because individual values were not extracted from primary tables.\n- The Amend and Shock per-valine free energies were not verified against the primary table and should be checked before quantitative use; the \u2076\u2070Co-HCN valine detection is from Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*).\n- Absence of a nonenzymatic acetolactate-pathway demonstration is based on targeted searches; an obscure report could exist.\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n2. [ChemInform Abstract: New Insights into Prebiotic Chemistry from Stanley Miller\u2032s Spark Discharge Experiments](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235337989_ChemInform_Abstract_New_Insights_into_Prebiotic_Chemistry_from_Stanley_Miller's_Spark_Discharge_Experiments)\n3. [(PDF) Abiotic synthesis of amino acids and self-crystallization under prebiotic conditions](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267743575_Abiotic_synthesis_of_amino_acids_and_self-crystallization_under_prebiotic_conditions)\n4. [Prebiotic synthesis in atmospheres containing CH4, CO, and CO2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02101642)\n5. [(PDF) A prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H2 and NH3 over nickel](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378533181_A_prebiotic_Krebs_cycle_analog_generates_amino_acids_with_H2_and_NH3_over_nickel)\n6. [Prebiotic Synthesis of N-Formylaminonitriles and Derivatives in Formamide](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c13306)\n7. [Prebiotic synthesis of \u03b1-amino acids and orotate from \u03b1-ketoacids potentiates transition to extant metabolic pathways](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-022-00999-w)\n8. [Primordial reductive amination revisited - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0040403902028630)\n9. [Laurie Barge, Ph.D. Senior Research Scientist, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory](https://kiss.resources.caltech.edu/uploads/2026/09/etorganics-talk-2-barge.pdf)\n10. [Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems](https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n11. [(PDF) Effects of Geochemical and Environmental Parameters on Abiotic Organic Chemistry Driven by Iron Hydroxide Minerals](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346847223_Effects_of_Geochemical_and_Environmental_Parameters_on_Abiotic_Organic_Chemistry_Driven_by_Iron_Hydroxide_Minerals)\n12. [Reaction Mechanisms Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid](https://d-nb.info/1270928767/34)\n13. [Schematic of the branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) biosynthesis pathway...](https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-branched-chain-amino-acid-BCAA-biosynthesis-pathway-in-R-eutropha_fig1_264429993)\n14. [Biosynthesis of the Branched-Chain Amino Acids H. E. UMBARGER 27 INTRODUCTION](https://ecosalgenes.frenoy.eu/pdf/782.pdf)\n\n\nKey Citations:\n  1. Prebiotic Synthesis of Hydrophobic and Protein Amino Acids (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.69.3.765)\n  2. ChemInform Abstract: New Insights into Prebiotic Chemistry from Stanley Miller\u2032s Spark Discharge Experiments (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235337989_ChemInform_Abstract_New_Insights_into_Prebiotic_Chemistry_from_Stanley_Miller's_Spark_Discharge_Experiments)\n  3. (PDF) Abiotic synthesis of amino acids and self-crystallization under prebiotic conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267743575_Abiotic_synthesis_of_amino_acids_and_self-crystallization_under_prebiotic_conditions)\n  4. Prebiotic synthesis in atmospheres containing CH4, CO, and CO2 (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02101642)\n  5. (PDF) A prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H2 and NH3 over nickel (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378533181_A_prebiotic_Krebs_cycle_analog_generates_amino_acids_with_H2_and_NH3_over_nickel)\n  6. Prebiotic Synthesis of N-Formylaminonitriles and Derivatives in Formamide (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/jacs.2c13306)\n  7. Prebiotic synthesis of \u03b1-amino acids and orotate from \u03b1-ketoacids potentiates transition to extant metabolic pathways (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-022-00999-w)\n  8. Primordial reductive amination revisited - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0040403902028630)\n  9. Laurie Barge, Ph.D. Senior Research Scientist, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (https://kiss.resources.caltech.edu/uploads/2026/09/etorganics-talk-2-barge.pdf)\n  10. Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems (https://www.pnas.org/doi/abs/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n  11. (PDF) Effects of Geochemical and Environmental Parameters on Abiotic Organic Chemistry Driven by Iron Hydroxide Minerals (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346847223_Effects_of_Geochemical_and_Environmental_Parameters_on_Abiotic_Organic_Chemistry_Driven_by_Iron_Hydroxide_Minerals)\n  12. Reaction Mechanisms Quantifying Reductive Amination in Nonenzymatic Amino Acid (https://d-nb.info/1270928767/34)\n  13. Schematic of the branched-chain amino acid (BCAA) biosynthesis pathway... (https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Schematic-of-the-branched-chain-amino-acid-BCAA-biosynthesis-pathway-in-R-eutropha_fig1_264429993)\n  14. Biosynthesis of the Branched-Chain Amino Acids H. E. UMBARGER 27 INTRODUCTION (https://ecosalgenes.frenoy.eu/pdf/782.pdf)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: The network is not approved at the network-level threshold of 0.9. Structural validity is high: the 31 molecule nodes and 25 reaction nodes form a connected graph, identifiers are consistent, and the target and major intermediates are appropriately distinguished. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, every reaction conserves atoms and charge with suitable coefficients; there are no conservation violations. Record the inferred coefficients explicitly for rxn_001, rxn_004, and rxn_022. The large coefficients in rxn_001 reflect a lumped mixed-product representation, not evidence for measured product ratios or a single mechanism.\n\nThe strongest supported route is P5, with P1, P2, and P4 also providing credible routes to racemic valine once operating conditions are supplied. The speculative alpha-ketoisovalerate branches do not block these alternatives, but cannot increase confidence merely by adding routes. Acetone and isobutyric acid are legitimate scoped boundary inputs; their lack of in-network synthesis is not a structural defect. Their availability and concentration remain environmental assumptions.\n\nThe complete network appropriately includes competing chemistry. rxn_017 is a supported ketoacid-reduction sink. rxn_020-rxn_023 form a chemically plausible, literature-aligned pantoate/pantolactone branch, although aldol catalysis, nitrile hydrolysis, and lactonization conditions need specification. rxn_024-rxn_025 are sensible net Strecker-plus-hydrolysis sequences; ammonia appearing on both sides does not remove the need for an ammonia-containing reaction medium. Mixed C5 amino-acid production is not itself failure to achieve valine, but requires analytical isomer resolution.\n\nEnvironmental consistency is the main network-wide implementation weakness. The supplied reaction nodes give categorical labels rather than temperatures, pressures, pH values, concentrations, catalyst forms, residence times, or transfer protocols. 'Biochemical' is not a defined physicochemical environment and does not establish an enzyme requirement. Missing details should be treated as uncertainty, not proof that all proposed settings are incompatible.\n\nLiterature alignment is good at the strategy level: the network correctly distinguishes demonstrated end-game chemistry, staged aminonitrile synthesis, conditional hydantoin routes, and speculative carboxylation. It also acknowledges important competing sinks and supply limitations. However, reaction-level citations and operational reasoning are absent. Attach primary evidence to the relevant nodes and distinguish observations from extrapolations. Problematic reaction lists include these implementation gaps as well as unsupported chemistry; none denotes an atom-balance violation.\n- Issues: ['The proposed aldehyde Bucherer\u2013Bergs route has sensible connectivity and correctly identified hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates. Its conditional designation is appropriate. However, formation of this particular hydantoin under the proposed environmental conditions is not established by the supplied evidence, and ring opening followed by complete decarbamoylation cannot be assumed to proceed efficiently in unspecified surface water.', 'The supported acetone homologation chemistry provides a credible entry to the aldehyde level, but diversion into hydantoin formation and subsequent hydrolysis remains conditional. The graph does not establish that the photochemical product mixture is compatible with hydantoin formation or that the resulting hydantoin and N-carbamoylvaline will release valine at useful rates. These are implementation and evidence gaps, not consequences of the number of stages.', \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate is strongly supported by Kaur et al. (2024). The complete pathway is nevertheless limited by rxn_013: no demonstrated abiotic activation/catalytic mechanism is provided for reductive carboxylation of unactivated isobutyric acid. Its balanced equation and hypothetical label do not establish kinetic feasibility. 'Biochemical' also does not specify the physical setting needed for the experimentally supported final reaction.\", 'The palladium-catalyzed final reaction is experimentally well supported, but improved downstream catalysis does not resolve the absent alpha-ketoisovalerate supply mechanism. The environmental limitation is not palladium chemistry itself: the graph provides no local source, catalyst form, support, or loading corresponding to the Pd/C laboratory demonstration.', 'Kaur et al. (2024) supports reductive amination using ground iron meteorite, so the final chemistry is credible without enzymes. The upstream reductive carboxylation remains unsupported, however, and meteorite occurrence alone does not establish the required reactive surface area, metal exposure, hydrogen supply, or catalyst persistence. The included hydroxy-acid branch appropriately acknowledges competing reduction.', 'This pathway combines the unsupported alpha-ketoisovalerate supply step with substrate-specific extrapolations of ketoacid hydantoin chemistry. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) supports the general reaction family, but the supplied evidence does not establish the proposed carboxylated valine hydantoin, its selective decarboxylation, and complete hydrolysis under the modeled conditions. The molecular bookkeeping is consistent; the shortcomings are experimental and mechanistic support.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.85\n- Feedback: The network has sufficient nodes, is connected and uses molecule IDs consistently, including separate IDs for constitutional isomers. The deterministic balance check establishes zero conservation violations. Reactions rxn_001, rxn_004, rxn_008, rxn_009 and rxn_016 require the reported coefficients; these should be recorded explicitly. The inferred discharge coefficients are formal aggregate bookkeeping, not measured product ratios. Declared boundary supplies are accepted: the unresolved alpha-ketoisovalerate origin limits origin claims but does not invalidate a conditional synthesis from that feedstock. The ten pathways are appropriately described as variants, and their number or environmental ordering does not increase the score. Established Strecker chemistry and the Kaur et al. reductive-amination experiments support actual racemic-valine production. Patel et al. supports the staged aminonitrile branch, without establishing a cumulative valine yield. The non-target branches are chemically coherent: rxn_013 represents experimentally supported competing carbonyl reduction; rxn_014-rxn_017 describe a plausible aldol/cyanohydrin/hydrolysis/lactonization sink, although catalyst and condition details are needed; rxn_018 and rxn_019 are legitimate composite Strecker-plus-hydrolysis reactions, including ammonia on both sides, but require distinct formation and hydrolysis conditions. The principal network-level weakness is that the supplied entries do not contain the quantitative reaction conditions and environmental implementation windows claimed in the strategy. Surface and Biochemical labels, together with anoxia, cannot establish catalyst availability, UV exposure, hydrogen activity, hydrolysis kinetics or material transfer. Separate staging is appropriate; independent boundary-fed branches need not be mixed. Where streams actually transfer, concentration, volatile losses and catalyst-poison removal need physical justification. Literature alignment is strong in substance, including acknowledgment of unresolved skeleton supply and competing sinks, but reaction-level citations are absent. Problematic-reaction flags identify under-specification or conditional evidence, not conservation failures. Overall confidence is below the 0.9 network-approval threshold, although all listed conditional pathways exceed the separate 0.8 pathway threshold.\n- Issues: []\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine (racemic; no stereoselective synthesis assumed)\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a connected, conditional synthesis network with 24 molecule nodes, 19 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction-sequence variants. The variants represent different precursor entry points, reagent staging, hydrolysis regimes or experimentally tested hydrogenation catalysts; they are not ten independently demonstrated origins of valine. The principal routes are isobutyraldehyde Strecker chemistry, the staged acetone-derived cyanosulfidic route, alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination, and experimentally observed discharge synthesis represented as a composite reaction. Acetone, isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovalerate are explicitly scoped boundary supplies. Their local availability is not established by declaring them starting materials; especially, no demonstrated abiotic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis is assumed. The unsupported nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway is excluded. Competing hydroxy-acid and pantolactone formation are included, and discharge-derived norvaline and isovaline require analytical separation from valine. All environments are anoxic. Surface chemistry and metal hydrogenation are separate implementations: cyanide- and sulfide-rich photochemical effluent is not assumed compatible with hydrogenation catalysts. Kaur et al. (2024) conditions are experimental benchmarks; other numerical windows are explicitly proposed implementation or screening conditions unless stated otherwise. Hot nitrile hydrolysis, volatile retention, precursor concentration and branched-skeleton supply remain important bottlenecks. Equations use neutral-parent molecular bookkeeping, with acid-base speciation and subsequent neutralization implicit. Stoichiometric coefficients are stated in mechanism fields, never encoded by repeating molecule IDs. Composite equations are atom-balanced projections, not measured product ratios. Because the role vocabulary lacks 'byproduct', non-target terminal products are labeled 'intermediate'. The environment_formed field identifies a principal formation setting; reaction nodes specify alternative settings.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Propan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetone\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-Methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NOS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetone-cyanohydrin-derived hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-Hydroxy-2,2-dimethylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxypivaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,4-Dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pantoic acid nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,4-Dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pantoic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-Hydroxy-4,4-dimethyloxolan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pantolactone\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Norvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-2-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isovaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-first formation of the transient isobutyraldehyde imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 8-9.5, 10-100 mM aldehyde and 50-500 mM total ammonia/ammonium, with hours-to-days residence. Use a sheltered, concentrating pool or gas-retaining pore; avoid sustained UV during aldehyde accumulation. Couple immediately to rxn_002 rather than isolating the imine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + NH3 -> isobutyraldehyde imine + H2O. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration establish a small, continuously trapped imine/iminium population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker synthesis of valine is supported by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). The explicit imine node is a mechanistic resolution of that reaction, not a claim that the supplied study isolated this intermediate. Water favors reversal, so imine accumulation is not assumed. The concentrations and residence window are proposed implementation conditions, not a quoted experimental recipe. Aldehyde supply and ammonia retention remain boundary assumptions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the imine to form valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Operate together with rxn_001 at pH 8-9.5. Proposed total cyanide concentration: 10-100 mM; residence: 1-7 days, assessed analytically rather than assumed sufficient. Retain volatile HCN in sheltered pores and distinguish aminonitrile from the competing cyanohydrin.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde imine + HCN -> valine aminonitrile. Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon; an achiral medium gives both configurations at the future alpha carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the established carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the valine Strecker route, consistent with Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Proposed millimolar cyanide requires local concentration or storage and release, not a homogeneous primitive ocean; the dilution problem is quantified by Stribling and Miller (1987). No mineral catalyst is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis by hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate hydrolysis stage: proposed pH 0-1 maintained after accounting for ammonia neutralization, 1-100 mM aminonitrile, and days-to-weeks residence. Protect from UV and monitor the intervening amide, remaining nitrile and decomposition products. This is an unoptimized surface window, not a measured valine-specific rate. Neutralization after hydrolysis gives the neutral-parent product represented here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: valine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> valine + NH3. Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide, followed by amide hydrolysis; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under reaction conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis completes conventional Strecker synthesis, including the valine chemistry discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Patel et al. (2015) supports access to the aminonitrile, not a cumulative acetone-to-valine yield under this proposed hydrolysis window. The supplied report does not establish a rate at these conditions. Strong acidity, buffering capacity and adequate residence are implementation requirements; neutral, cool water cannot be assumed to accomplish rapid conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline solution; base promoter, not a conserved catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alternative proposed stage: pH 11-12 at reaction temperature, 1-100 mM aminonitrile and days-to-weeks residence in an evaporatively concentrated alkaline compartment. Maintain alkalinity as carboxylate accumulates. Monitor incomplete amide hydrolysis and degradation. Subsequent neutralization is implicit in neutral-parent bookkeeping; this is not a claim that hydroxide is catalytic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Neutral-parent net equation: valine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> valine + NH3. Hydroxide-promoted hydration and amide cleavage initially produce valinate; protonation during later equilibration yields the represented parent acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The underlying nitrile-to-amino-acid conversion is standard Strecker chemistry, supported at the route level by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). This particular alkaline geothermal implementation is an extrapolation, not a valine-specific demonstration in the supplied research. It is included as an alternative hydrolysis regime where strong acid is unavailable, with completion and useful yield explicitly unresolved. P1/P2 and analogous pairs are operational variants, not independent reaction families.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-delayed capture of isobutyraldehyde as its cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM aldehyde and total cyanide, low ammonia during initial capture, and 1-24 hours before testing ammonia addition. Avoid prolonged heating because aldehyde and HCN are volatile and cyanohydrin formation is reversible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + HCN -> isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin. Cyanide addition to the carbonyl followed by protonation establishes a reversible carbonyl-cyanohydrin equilibrium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin intermediates and subsequent ammonia capture are consistent with the valine branch in Patel et al. (2015) and the Strecker chemistry reviewed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). This separates an ammonia-delayed implementation from ammonia-first mixing; it does not assert fundamentally unrelated mechanisms. The proposed timing and concentrations require validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Conversion of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 8-9.5, 10-100 mM cyanohydrin and 50-500 mM total ammonia/ammonium, with 1-7 days monitored residence. For photochemical material, stop irradiation and separate settled sulfur/copper-containing solids before testing this stage. Do not assume quantitative intermediate recovery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + NH3 -> valine aminonitrile + H2O. Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, imine formation and cyanide recapture provide a credible pathway; direct displacement of hydroxide is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated ammonia-dependent access to the valine aminonitrile in the staged cyanosulfidic network. The supplied report gives no cumulative valine yield. Natural delivery of concentrated ammonia, competing cyanohydrin hydrolysis, volatile losses and recovery from photochemical mixtures remain unresolved. The numerical window here is a proposed implementation rather than a reconstruction of the paper's protocol.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM acetone and total cyanide, and 1-24 hours of equilibration. A sheltered pool or pore must limit acetone and HCN loss. Quantify the equilibrium mixture before sulfide addition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: acetone + HCN -> acetone cyanohydrin. Nucleophilic carbonyl addition extends the C3 ketone to a C4 cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The acetone entry to the valine-precursor branch is supported by Patel et al. (2015). Acetone is an explicitly supplied small-organic feedstock here; its environmental concentration is not established. The reaction is reversible, and no isolated or cumulative yield is inferred from the literature summary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM cyanohydrin and 10-100 mM total dissolved sulfide, with hours-to-days residence before irradiation. Sulfide is a reactant, not a catalyst. Retain dissolved sulfide and quantify its removal by FeS precipitation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: acetone cyanohydrin + H2S -> hydroxythioamide. Sulfide addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfer yields the corresponding thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This intermediate class is part of the valine-relevant sequence described by Patel et al. (2015). The exact screening window is proposed, not reported in the supplied summary. Sulfide abundance is a major geochemical restriction: Ranjan et al. (2018) cautions that ordinary volcanic outgassing does not readily supply the required H2S-derived anions. Abundant Fe2+ also competes for sulfide, so a locally replenished sulfide source is necessary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photoreductive homologation and cyanohydrin trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Copper(I)/copper(II) cyanide-containing photoredox catalyst system\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)/copper(II) photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm irradiation is the laboratory analogue. Proposed screening window: pH 7-9, 10-100 mM organic substrate and total cyanide, 10-100 mM sulfide, 0.1-1 mM total copper, and 1-24 hours of monitored exposure. These are design values, not reported optima. Use a shallow irradiated compartment; quantify optical attenuation, copper precipitation and sulfur accumulation. Subsequent ammonia addition and hydrolysis occur in separate stages.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal net module: 4 hydroxythioamide + 4 H2S + 4 HCN -> 4 isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + 4 NH3 + S8. The module combines reductive deoxygenation, conversion to the aldehyde oxidation level and cyanide trapping; it is not an elementary step. S8 represents an idealized sulfur(0) endpoint, not an experimentally established exclusive sulfur product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports staged access from acetone-derived intermediates to the valine aminonitrile. The present node compresses its photochemical subnetwork and does not claim isolation of free isobutyraldehyde or an established sulfur-product distribution. Polysulfides and other sulfur species can replace the idealized S8 sink in a more detailed model. Ranjan et al. (2018) identifies sulfide supply as a serious limitation. Catalyst speciation, staged delivery and spectral differences between 254 nm irradiation and sunlight prevent treating this as a demonstrated natural one-pot synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Nickel metal supported on silica-alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. Biochemical denotes enzyme-free molecular assembly at a mineral interface, not an enzyme requirement. A natural analogue would require an H2-retaining, cooled pore with accessible native nickel; the manufactured support is not claimed to be a natural mineral assemblage. Keep cyanide- and sulfide-rich feeds separate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Carbonyl-ammonia condensation is followed by metal-catalyzed hydrogenation of the imine/iminium intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine formation from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate under these benchmark conditions is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024); no reduction occurred without a metal catalyst. Native Ni or Fe-Ni surfaces are geochemical analogues, not demonstrated replacements for this particular supported catalyst. Precursor supply remains unresolved, and direct carbonyl reduction competes through rxn_013.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Palladium supported on carbon\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Pd/C, 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. Use an independent, sulfide- and cyanide-poor reaction compartment. Pd/C is retained as an experimentally supported comparison, not assumed to have been widely available on early Earth.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Palladium activates H2 and reduces the imine/iminium formed from the ketoacid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly supports the palladium-catalyzed end-game and generally higher amino-acid selectivity than nickel across the tested substrates. The reported substrate-wide yield and selectivity ranges are not assigned here as valine-specific values. Palladium rarity and the unestablished origin of an effective carbon-supported catalyst make this a chemically strong but geochemically conditional variant; it does not solve alpha-ketoisovalerate supply.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ground Aletai iron meteorite with exposed Fe-Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ground Aletai meteorite; reported loading approximately 18 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. The cited experiment used 3 mg meteorite at its published reactor scale; scale catalyst and substrate together rather than treating 3 mg as volume-independent. A natural implementation requires comminuted, freshly exposed metal, retained H2 and limited surface passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Accessible metal sites activate hydrogen and catalyze reduction of the ketoacid-derived imine/iminium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) directly demonstrated the meteorite-catalyzed reaction, giving this branch stronger substrate-specific support than extrapolations from green-rust alanine chemistry. Meteorite delivery alone does not establish adequate surface area, metal exposure, H2 activity or catalyst lifetime. Alpha-ketoisovalerate remains a supplied boundary substrate, and hydroxy-acid competition is represented by rxn_013.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to its hydroxy acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or exposed Fe-Ni in ground Aletai meteorite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Same nickel or meteorite catalyst used in rxn_010 or rxn_012\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Competing branch under the benchmark 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72-hour conditions. Ammonia can be present without appearing in the net equation. Quantify amino acid and hydroxy acid together; do not assign a fixed branching ratio.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + H2 -> alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid. Direct reduction of the ketone competes with imine formation and hydrogenation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reports hydroxy acids as competing products, particularly with nickel and meteorite catalysts. The approximately comparable amino-acid and hydroxy-acid production reported across those experiments is not imposed as a valine-specific ratio. This experimentally motivated sink prevents assuming quantitative conversion of supplied ketoacid to valine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrolysis of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis by hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 0-1 and days-to-weeks residence if cyanohydrin-containing material enters the hydrolysis compartment before effective amination. Compare hydrolysis against cyanohydrin dissociation and volatile loss. Ammonia released is protonated under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + 2 H2O -> alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid + NH3. Nitrile hydrolysis retains the alpha hydroxyl group instead of installing an amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a chemically expected cyanohydrin-hydrolysis sink rather than a demonstrated valine-specific kinetic result in the supplied research. It is an explicit mechanistic extrapolation from the cyanohydrin/Strecker chemistry discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). Its purpose is to expose the cost of incomplete ammonia conversion before hot hydrolysis; no yield is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate-assisted diversion of isobutyraldehyde by formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved inorganic phosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Phosphate general acid-base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed implementation: pH 7-8, 10-100 mM carbonyl substrates and phosphate, with hours-to-days residence. A phosphate-rich compartment requires local concentration and limited removal by iron or calcium precipitation. Formaldehyde availability controls competition with valine precursor capture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + formaldehyde -> hydroxypivaldehyde. Enolization of isobutyraldehyde followed by aldol addition installs a hydroxymethyl group and consumes the aldehyde's only alpha hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fairchild et al. (2024) supports phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol diversion of these carbonyl precursors toward pantolactone/pantetheine chemistry. Miller and Schlesinger (1993) supports prebiotic pantoic-acid formation from the same aldehyde/formaldehyde/cyanide combination. The specified concentration and residence ranges are proposed, not extracted yields or optimized conditions. Phosphate is justified by its demonstrated catalytic function, not added as a generic mineral.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation on the pantoate branch\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM hydroxypivaldehyde and total cyanide, with hours-to-days equilibration. Limit HCN loss. This branch competes for the same cyanide pool as valine aminonitrile formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: hydroxypivaldehyde + HCN -> pantoic acid nitrile. Cyanide addition to the aldehyde generates the alpha-hydroxynitrile corresponding to pantoic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller and Schlesinger (1993) supports the overall isobutyraldehyde/formaldehyde/HCN route to pantoic acid. This node resolves its chemically plausible cyanohydrin step without claiming an independently measured rate or yield. Fairchild et al. (2024) provides additional support for diversion of the same carbonyl feedstocks, not for every condition specified here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of pantoic acid nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis by hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate proposed stage: pH 0-1 maintained against ammonia release, 1-100 mM nitrile and days-to-weeks residence. Monitor amide accumulation, pantoic acid and concurrent lactonization. The modeled hydrolysis window has no assigned completion time or yield.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: pantoic acid nitrile + 2 H2O -> pantoic acid + NH3. Sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis forms the hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The overall pantoic-acid synthesis is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993). The explicit intermediate resolution and lower-temperature geothermal residence window are mechanistic and environmental extrapolations. This branch is not assumed to hydrolyze rapidly in neutral surface water, addressing an important implementation limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Pantoic acid lactonization during acidic concentration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic solution providing proton catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 1-3 with hours-to-days of evaporation or wet-dry cycling. The aqueous flag denotes the initial concentrated solution; later partial drying lowers water activity. Rewetting can reopen the lactone, so pantolactone is not an irreversible sink under all conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: pantoic acid -> pantolactone + H2O. Intramolecular esterification between the carboxyl group and terminal hydroxyl forms a five-membered lactone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pantolactone formation is consistent with the pantoate chemistry of Miller and Schlesinger (1993) and the carbonyl-diversion network of Fairchild et al. (2024). Five-membered lactone formation is chemically favorable, but partitioning depends on pH and water activity. The proposed cycling conditions are not a claim of a measured environmental yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Composite reducing-atmosphere discharge synthesis and aqueous workup\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K bulk gas-liquid apparatus; discharge channels are locally much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar initial gas pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Methane-rich, ammonia-containing, oxygen-free gas over a circulating aqueous condensate trap, energized by electrical discharge. A proposed implementation uses 1-7 days of discharge followed by a separate acidic hydrolysis stage, screened at 353-370 K and pH 0-1 for days to weeks. These are design windows, not a reconstruction of the cited assays. Preserve the distinction between precursor production and free amino acids released during workup. Resolve valine, norvaline and isovaline chromatographically.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal balanced projection: 15 CH4 + 3 NH3 + 6 H2O -> valine + norvaline + isovaline + 24 H2. This composite node covers radical gas chemistry, aqueous capture and hydrolysis, not a single elementary reaction. The coefficients merely balance the selected products; they do not imply equal amino-acid yields, measured hydrogen evolution or an exhaustive product inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Valine and its constitutional isomers were identified in discharge products by Ring et al. (1972), with additional support from the archived-sample analyses described by Johnson et al. (2009). This observation supports the composite route without establishing every transient carbonyl or aminonitrile. Schlesinger and Miller (1983) shows strong dependence on reducing gas composition, so a globally CO2/N2-dominated atmosphere cannot be substituted without qualification. Natural relevance is conditional on locally reducing plumes or transient atmospheres, efficient collection and a credible hydrolysis environment; no selective valine yield is claimed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-first Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied isobutyraldehyde undergoes coupled imine formation and cyanide capture, followed by transfer to an acidic hydrolysis compartment. The end-game chemistry is established; aldehyde supply, concentration and the proposed hydrolysis residence remain conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-first Strecker route with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the same carbon-skeleton assembly as P1 but a separate alkaline hydrolysis and neutralization stage. This is an operationally distinct pH variant, not another independently demonstrated primordial reaction family; the proposed alkaline implementation requires validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied isobutyraldehyde is initially captured with cyanide before ammonia delivery. The resulting aminonitrile is hydrolyzed in a separate acidic compartment. Delayed ammonia is a feed-staging variant of Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first Strecker route with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines delayed ammonia capture with the alternative alkaline hydrolysis regime. Cyanohydrin hydrolysis before amination competes through rxn_014, and the final alkaline conversion has no demonstrated rate under the proposed surface conditions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone-derived cyanosulfidic route with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Builds the required branched precursor from supplied acetone through cyanohydrin and hydroxythioamide chemistry, photoreductive homologation, ammonia capture and acidic nitrile hydrolysis. The staged aminonitrile route is literature-supported; natural staging, sulfur balance, cumulative recovery and the final valine yield are not established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone-derived cyanosulfidic route with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Shares the demonstrated staged aminonitrile-access chemistry of P5 but tests alkaline rather than acidic release of valine. It inherits the sulfide, irradiation and transfer limitations and adds an unvalidated alkaline hydrolysis implementation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with supported nickel\",\n      \"description\": \"An experimentally supported, enzyme-free end-game using the quantified nickel benchmark. It starts from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate rather than inventing an abiotic skeleton-building step. Hydroxy-acid production competes, and equivalence between supported laboratory nickel and natural surfaces is not assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with palladium on carbon\",\n      \"description\": \"An experimentally supported catalytic variant with generally improved amino-acid selectivity. It remains conditional on precursor supply and a locally available palladium catalyst and is retained as a laboratory-supported comparison rather than a preferred geochemical scenario.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the demonstrated ground-meteorite catalyst implementation. Natural comminution, exposed metal, hydrogen retention and resistance to passivation must be established independently; meteoritic occurrence does not by itself supply these conditions or the ketoacid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A complete experimentally motivated feedstock-to-product entry represented by one explicitly composite node. Methane, ammonia and water yield a complex precursor mixture from which valine is detected after aqueous processing, accompanied by norvaline and isovaline. The pathway is nonselective, and the proposed lower-temperature geological hydrolysis window is not identical to a demonstrated analytical workup.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_018\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine (racemic; no stereoselective synthesis assumed)", "overall_strategy": "Construct a connected, conditional synthesis network with 24 molecule nodes, 19 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction-sequence variants. The variants represent different precursor entry points, reagent staging, hydrolysis regimes or experimentally tested hydrogenation catalysts; they are not ten independently demonstrated origins of valine. The principal routes are isobutyraldehyde Strecker chemistry, the staged acetone-derived cyanosulfidic route, alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination, and experimentally observed discharge synthesis represented as a composite reaction. Acetone, isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovalerate are explicitly scoped boundary supplies. Their local availability is not established by declaring them starting materials; especially, no demonstrated abiotic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis is assumed. The unsupported nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway is excluded. Competing hydroxy-acid and pantolactone formation are included, and discharge-derived norvaline and isovaline require analytical separation from valine. All environments are anoxic. Surface chemistry and metal hydrogenation are separate implementations: cyanide- and sulfide-rich photochemical effluent is not assumed compatible with hydrogenation catalysts. Kaur et al. (2024) conditions are experimental benchmarks; other numerical windows are explicitly proposed implementation or screening conditions unless stated otherwise. Hot nitrile hydrolysis, volatile retention, precursor concentration and branched-skeleton supply remain important bottlenecks. Equations use neutral-parent molecular bookkeeping, with acid-base speciation and subsequent neutralization implicit. Stoichiometric coefficients are stated in mechanism fields, never encoded by repeating molecule IDs. Composite equations are atom-balanced projections, not measured product ratios. Because the role vocabulary lacks 'byproduct', non-target terminal products are labeled 'intermediate'. The environment_formed field identifies a principal formation setting; reaction nodes specify alternative settings.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "Propan-2-one", "common_name": "Acetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-Methylpropanal", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-Methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "Methane", "common_name": "Methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-Methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Valine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile", "common_name": "Acetone cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C4H9NOS", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide", "common_name": "Acetone-cyanohydrin-derived hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H10O3", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C5H10O2", "iupac_name": "3-Hydroxy-2,2-dimethylpropanal", "common_name": "Hydroxypivaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2,4-Dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Pantoic acid nitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C6H12O4", "iupac_name": "2,4-Dihydroxy-3,3-dimethylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Pantoic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C6H10O3", "iupac_name": "3-Hydroxy-4,4-dimethyloxolan-2-one", "common_name": "Pantolactone", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "Norvaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-2-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "Isovaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Ammonia-first formation of the transient isobutyraldehyde imine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 8-9.5, 10-100 mM aldehyde and 50-500 mM total ammonia/ammonium, with hours-to-days residence. Use a sheltered, concentrating pool or gas-retaining pore; avoid sustained UV during aldehyde accumulation. Couple immediately to rxn_002 rather than isolating the imine."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + NH3 -> isobutyraldehyde imine + H2O. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration establish a small, continuously trapped imine/iminium population.", "reasoning": "Strecker synthesis of valine is supported by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). The explicit imine node is a mechanistic resolution of that reaction, not a claim that the supplied study isolated this intermediate. Water favors reversal, so imine accumulation is not assumed. The concentrations and residence window are proposed implementation conditions, not a quoted experimental recipe. Aldehyde supply and ammonia retention remain boundary assumptions."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide capture of the imine to form valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Operate together with rxn_001 at pH 8-9.5. Proposed total cyanide concentration: 10-100 mM; residence: 1-7 days, assessed analytically rather than assumed sufficient. Retain volatile HCN in sheltered pores and distinguish aminonitrile from the competing cyanohydrin."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde imine + HCN -> valine aminonitrile. Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon; an achiral medium gives both configurations at the future alpha carbon.", "reasoning": "This is the established carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the valine Strecker route, consistent with Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Proposed millimolar cyanide requires local concentration or storage and release, not a homogeneous primitive ocean; the dilution problem is quantified by Stribling and Miller (1987). No mineral catalyst is required."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Acid-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis by hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate hydrolysis stage: proposed pH 0-1 maintained after accounting for ammonia neutralization, 1-100 mM aminonitrile, and days-to-weeks residence. Protect from UV and monitor the intervening amide, remaining nitrile and decomposition products. This is an unoptimized surface window, not a measured valine-specific rate. Neutralization after hydrolysis gives the neutral-parent product represented here."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: valine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> valine + NH3. Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration proceeds through an amide, followed by amide hydrolysis; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under reaction conditions.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis completes conventional Strecker synthesis, including the valine chemistry discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Patel et al. (2015) supports access to the aminonitrile, not a cumulative acetone-to-valine yield under this proposed hydrolysis window. The supplied report does not establish a rate at these conditions. Strong acidity, buffering capacity and adequate residence are implementation requirements; neutral, cool water cannot be assumed to accomplish rapid conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Base-promoted hydrolysis of valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline solution; base promoter, not a conserved catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Alternative proposed stage: pH 11-12 at reaction temperature, 1-100 mM aminonitrile and days-to-weeks residence in an evaporatively concentrated alkaline compartment. Maintain alkalinity as carboxylate accumulates. Monitor incomplete amide hydrolysis and degradation. Subsequent neutralization is implicit in neutral-parent bookkeeping; this is not a claim that hydroxide is catalytic."}, "mechanism": "Neutral-parent net equation: valine aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> valine + NH3. Hydroxide-promoted hydration and amide cleavage initially produce valinate; protonation during later equilibration yields the represented parent acid.", "reasoning": "The underlying nitrile-to-amino-acid conversion is standard Strecker chemistry, supported at the route level by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). This particular alkaline geothermal implementation is an extrapolation, not a valine-specific demonstration in the supplied research. It is included as an alternative hydrolysis regime where strong acid is unavailable, with completion and useful yield explicitly unresolved. P1/P2 and analogous pairs are operational variants, not independent reaction families."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ammonia-delayed capture of isobutyraldehyde as its cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM aldehyde and total cyanide, low ammonia during initial capture, and 1-24 hours before testing ammonia addition. Avoid prolonged heating because aldehyde and HCN are volatile and cyanohydrin formation is reversible."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + HCN -> isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin. Cyanide addition to the carbonyl followed by protonation establishes a reversible carbonyl-cyanohydrin equilibrium.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin intermediates and subsequent ammonia capture are consistent with the valine branch in Patel et al. (2015) and the Strecker chemistry reviewed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). This separates an ammonia-delayed implementation from ammonia-first mixing; it does not assert fundamentally unrelated mechanisms. The proposed timing and concentrations require validation."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Conversion of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin to valine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 8-9.5, 10-100 mM cyanohydrin and 50-500 mM total ammonia/ammonium, with 1-7 days monitored residence. For photochemical material, stop irradiation and separate settled sulfur/copper-containing solids before testing this stage. Do not assume quantitative intermediate recovery."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + NH3 -> valine aminonitrile + H2O. Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, imine formation and cyanide recapture provide a credible pathway; direct displacement of hydroxide is not assumed.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated ammonia-dependent access to the valine aminonitrile in the staged cyanosulfidic network. The supplied report gives no cumulative valine yield. Natural delivery of concentrated ammonia, competing cyanohydrin hydrolysis, volatile losses and recovery from photochemical mixtures remain unresolved. The numerical window here is a proposed implementation rather than a reconstruction of the paper's protocol."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM acetone and total cyanide, and 1-24 hours of equilibration. A sheltered pool or pore must limit acetone and HCN loss. Quantify the equilibrium mixture before sulfide addition."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: acetone + HCN -> acetone cyanohydrin. Nucleophilic carbonyl addition extends the C3 ketone to a C4 cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "The acetone entry to the valine-precursor branch is supported by Patel et al. (2015). Acetone is an explicitly supplied small-organic feedstock here; its environmental concentration is not established. The reaction is reversible, and no isolated or cumulative yield is inferred from the literature summary."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM cyanohydrin and 10-100 mM total dissolved sulfide, with hours-to-days residence before irradiation. Sulfide is a reactant, not a catalyst. Retain dissolved sulfide and quantify its removal by FeS precipitation."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: acetone cyanohydrin + H2S -> hydroxythioamide. Sulfide addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfer yields the corresponding thioamide.", "reasoning": "This intermediate class is part of the valine-relevant sequence described by Patel et al. (2015). The exact screening window is proposed, not reported in the supplied summary. Sulfide abundance is a major geochemical restriction: Ranjan et al. (2018) cautions that ordinary volcanic outgassing does not readily supply the required H2S-derived anions. Abundant Fe2+ also competes for sulfide, so a locally replenished sulfide source is necessary."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanosulfidic photoreductive homologation and cyanohydrin trapping", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Copper(I)/copper(II) cyanide-containing photoredox catalyst system"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)/copper(II) photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm irradiation is the laboratory analogue. Proposed screening window: pH 7-9, 10-100 mM organic substrate and total cyanide, 10-100 mM sulfide, 0.1-1 mM total copper, and 1-24 hours of monitored exposure. These are design values, not reported optima. Use a shallow irradiated compartment; quantify optical attenuation, copper precipitation and sulfur accumulation. Subsequent ammonia addition and hydrolysis occur in separate stages."}, "mechanism": "Formal net module: 4 hydroxythioamide + 4 H2S + 4 HCN -> 4 isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + 4 NH3 + S8. The module combines reductive deoxygenation, conversion to the aldehyde oxidation level and cyanide trapping; it is not an elementary step. S8 represents an idealized sulfur(0) endpoint, not an experimentally established exclusive sulfur product.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) supports staged access from acetone-derived intermediates to the valine aminonitrile. The present node compresses its photochemical subnetwork and does not claim isolation of free isobutyraldehyde or an established sulfur-product distribution. Polysulfides and other sulfur species can replace the idealized S8 sink in a more detailed model. Ranjan et al. (2018) identifies sulfide supply as a serious limitation. Catalyst speciation, staged delivery and spectral differences between 254 nm irradiation and sunlight prevent treating this as a demonstrated natural one-pot synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Nickel metal supported on silica-alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, 10 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. Biochemical denotes enzyme-free molecular assembly at a mineral interface, not an enzyme requirement. A natural analogue would require an H2-retaining, cooled pore with accessible native nickel; the manufactured support is not claimed to be a natural mineral assemblage. Keep cyanide- and sulfide-rich feeds separate."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Carbonyl-ammonia condensation is followed by metal-catalyzed hydrogenation of the imine/iminium intermediate.", "reasoning": "Valine formation from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate under these benchmark conditions is directly supported by Kaur et al. (2024); no reduction occurred without a metal catalyst. Native Ni or Fe-Ni surfaces are geochemical analogues, not demonstrated replacements for this particular supported catalyst. Precursor supply remains unresolved, and direct carbonyl reduction competes through rxn_013."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Palladium-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Palladium supported on carbon"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C, 1 mol% Pd relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. Use an independent, sulfide- and cyanide-poor reaction compartment. Pd/C is retained as an experimentally supported comparison, not assumed to have been widely available on early Earth."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Palladium activates H2 and reduces the imine/iminium formed from the ketoacid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly supports the palladium-catalyzed end-game and generally higher amino-acid selectivity than nickel across the tested substrates. The reported substrate-wide yield and selectivity ranges are not assigned here as valine-specific values. Palladium rarity and the unestablished origin of an effective carbon-supported catalyst make this a chemically strong but geochemically conditional variant; it does not solve alpha-ketoisovalerate supply."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Iron-meteorite-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ground Aletai iron meteorite with exposed Fe-Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ground Aletai meteorite; reported loading approximately 18 mol% Ni relative to ketoacid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Experimental benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72 hours. The cited experiment used 3 mg meteorite at its published reactor scale; scale catalyst and substrate together rather than treating 3 mg as volume-independent. A natural implementation requires comminuted, freshly exposed metal, retained H2 and limited surface passivation."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + NH3 + H2 -> valine + H2O. Accessible metal sites activate hydrogen and catalyze reduction of the ketoacid-derived imine/iminium.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly demonstrated the meteorite-catalyzed reaction, giving this branch stronger substrate-specific support than extrapolations from green-rust alanine chemistry. Meteorite delivery alone does not establish adequate surface area, metal exposure, H2 activity or catalyst lifetime. Alpha-ketoisovalerate remains a supplied boundary substrate, and hydroxy-acid competition is represented by rxn_013."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Competing hydrogenation of alpha-ketoisovalerate to its hydroxy acid", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or exposed Fe-Ni in ground Aletai meteorite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Same nickel or meteorite catalyst used in rxn_010 or rxn_012", "aqueous": true, "other": "Competing branch under the benchmark 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, pH 8 and 72-hour conditions. Ammonia can be present without appearing in the net equation. Quantify amino acid and hydroxy acid together; do not assign a fixed branching ratio."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: alpha-ketoisovaleric acid + H2 -> alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid. Direct reduction of the ketone competes with imine formation and hydrogenation.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reports hydroxy acids as competing products, particularly with nickel and meteorite catalysts. The approximately comparable amino-acid and hydroxy-acid production reported across those experiments is not imposed as a valine-specific ratio. This experimentally motivated sink prevents assuming quantitative conversion of supplied ketoacid to valine."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Competing hydrolysis of isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis by hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 0-1 and days-to-weeks residence if cyanohydrin-containing material enters the hydrolysis compartment before effective amination. Compare hydrolysis against cyanohydrin dissociation and volatile loss. Ammonia released is protonated under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin + 2 H2O -> alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid + NH3. Nitrile hydrolysis retains the alpha hydroxyl group instead of installing an amino group.", "reasoning": "This is a chemically expected cyanohydrin-hydrolysis sink rather than a demonstrated valine-specific kinetic result in the supplied research. It is an explicit mechanistic extrapolation from the cyanohydrin/Strecker chemistry discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). Its purpose is to expose the cost of incomplete ammonia conversion before hot hydrolysis; no yield is asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Phosphate-assisted diversion of isobutyraldehyde by formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved inorganic phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Phosphate general acid-base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed implementation: pH 7-8, 10-100 mM carbonyl substrates and phosphate, with hours-to-days residence. A phosphate-rich compartment requires local concentration and limited removal by iron or calcium precipitation. Formaldehyde availability controls competition with valine precursor capture."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: isobutyraldehyde + formaldehyde -> hydroxypivaldehyde. Enolization of isobutyraldehyde followed by aldol addition installs a hydroxymethyl group and consumes the aldehyde's only alpha hydrogen.", "reasoning": "Fairchild et al. (2024) supports phosphate-catalyzed, neutral-pH aldol diversion of these carbonyl precursors toward pantolactone/pantetheine chemistry. Miller and Schlesinger (1993) supports prebiotic pantoic-acid formation from the same aldehyde/formaldehyde/cyanide combination. The specified concentration and residence ranges are proposed, not extracted yields or optimized conditions. Phosphate is justified by its demonstrated catalytic function, not added as a generic mineral."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation on the pantoate branch", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7.5-9, 10-100 mM hydroxypivaldehyde and total cyanide, with hours-to-days equilibration. Limit HCN loss. This branch competes for the same cyanide pool as valine aminonitrile formation."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: hydroxypivaldehyde + HCN -> pantoic acid nitrile. Cyanide addition to the aldehyde generates the alpha-hydroxynitrile corresponding to pantoic acid.", "reasoning": "Miller and Schlesinger (1993) supports the overall isobutyraldehyde/formaldehyde/HCN route to pantoic acid. This node resolves its chemically plausible cyanohydrin step without claiming an independently measured rate or yield. Fairchild et al. (2024) provides additional support for diversion of the same carbonyl feedstocks, not for every condition specified here."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Hydrolysis of pantoic acid nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium in an acidic geothermal solution"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis by hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate proposed stage: pH 0-1 maintained against ammonia release, 1-100 mM nitrile and days-to-weeks residence. Monitor amide accumulation, pantoic acid and concurrent lactonization. The modeled hydrolysis window has no assigned completion time or yield."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: pantoic acid nitrile + 2 H2O -> pantoic acid + NH3. Sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis forms the hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "The overall pantoic-acid synthesis is supported by Miller and Schlesinger (1993). The explicit intermediate resolution and lower-temperature geothermal residence window are mechanistic and environmental extrapolations. This branch is not assumed to hydrolyze rapidly in neutral surface water, addressing an important implementation limitation."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Pantoic acid lactonization during acidic concentration", "inputs": ["mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic solution providing proton catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-3 with hours-to-days of evaporation or wet-dry cycling. The aqueous flag denotes the initial concentrated solution; later partial drying lowers water activity. Rewetting can reopen the lactone, so pantolactone is not an irreversible sink under all conditions."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: pantoic acid -> pantolactone + H2O. Intramolecular esterification between the carboxyl group and terminal hydroxyl forms a five-membered lactone.", "reasoning": "Pantolactone formation is consistent with the pantoate chemistry of Miller and Schlesinger (1993) and the carbonyl-diversion network of Fairchild et al. (2024). Five-membered lactone formation is chemically favorable, but partitioning depends on pH and water activity. The proposed cycling conditions are not a claim of a measured environmental yield."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Composite reducing-atmosphere discharge synthesis and aqueous workup", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_023", "mol_024", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K bulk gas-liquid apparatus; discharge channels are locally much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar initial gas pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Methane-rich, ammonia-containing, oxygen-free gas over a circulating aqueous condensate trap, energized by electrical discharge. A proposed implementation uses 1-7 days of discharge followed by a separate acidic hydrolysis stage, screened at 353-370 K and pH 0-1 for days to weeks. These are design windows, not a reconstruction of the cited assays. Preserve the distinction between precursor production and free amino acids released during workup. Resolve valine, norvaline and isovaline chromatographically."}, "mechanism": "Formal balanced projection: 15 CH4 + 3 NH3 + 6 H2O -> valine + norvaline + isovaline + 24 H2. This composite node covers radical gas chemistry, aqueous capture and hydrolysis, not a single elementary reaction. The coefficients merely balance the selected products; they do not imply equal amino-acid yields, measured hydrogen evolution or an exhaustive product inventory.", "reasoning": "Valine and its constitutional isomers were identified in discharge products by Ring et al. (1972), with additional support from the archived-sample analyses described by Johnson et al. (2009). This observation supports the composite route without establishing every transient carbonyl or aminonitrile. Schlesinger and Miller (1983) shows strong dependence on reducing gas composition, so a globally CO2/N2-dominated atmosphere cannot be substituted without qualification. Natural relevance is conditional on locally reducing plumes or transient atmospheres, efficient collection and a credible hydrolysis environment; no selective valine yield is claimed."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Ammonia-first Strecker route with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Supplied isobutyraldehyde undergoes coupled imine formation and cyanide capture, followed by transfer to an acidic hydrolysis compartment. The end-game chemistry is established; aldehyde supply, concentration and the proposed hydrolysis residence remain conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Ammonia-first Strecker route with