", "description": "Shares the demonstrated staged aminonitrile-access chemistry of P5 but tests alkaline rather than acidic release of valine. It inherits the sulfide, irradiation and transfer limitations and adds an unvalidated alkaline hydrolysis implementation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_006", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_014", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with supported nickel", "description": "An experimentally supported, enzyme-free end-game using the quantified nickel benchmark. It starts from supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate rather than inventing an abiotic skeleton-building step. Hydroxy-acid production competes, and equivalence between supported laboratory nickel and natural surfaces is not assumed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with palladium on carbon", "description": "An experimentally supported catalytic variant with generally improved amino-acid selectivity. It remains conditional on precursor supply and a locally available palladium catalyst and is retained as a laboratory-supported comparison rather than a preferred geochemical scenario.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate with exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni", "description": "Uses the demonstrated ground-meteorite catalyst implementation. Natural comminution, exposed metal, hydrogen retention and resistance to passivation must be established independently; meteoritic occurrence does not by itself supply these conditions or the ketoacid.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Reducing-atmosphere discharge and aqueous hydrolysis", "description": "A complete experimentally motivated feedstock-to-product entry represented by one explicitly composite node. Methane, ammonia and water yield a complex precursor mixture from which valine is detected after aqueous processing, accompanied by norvaline and isovaline. The pathway is nonselective, and the proposed lower-temperature geological hydrolysis window is not identical to a demonstrated analytical workup.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_019"], "key_intermediates": []}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_018"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_014", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_014"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_018", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_013", "rxn_014"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_013", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_019"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 370749.0577697754, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T16:06:26.587410", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine (racemic; no stereoselective synthesis assumed)\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct a connected, conditional synthesis network with 24 molecule nodes, 19 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction-sequence variants. The variants represent different precursor entry points, reagent staging, hydrolysis regimes or experimentally tested hydrogenation catalysts; they are not ten independently demonstrated origins of valine. The principal routes are isobutyraldehyde Strecker chemistry, the staged acetone-derived cyanosulfidic route, alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination, and experimentally observed discharge synthesis represented as a composite reaction. Acetone, isobutyraldehyde and alpha-ketoisovalerate are explicitly scoped boundary supplies. Their local availability is not established by declaring them starting materials; especially, no demonstrated abiotic alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis is assumed. The unsupported nonenzymatic acetolactate pathway is excluded. Competing hydroxy-acid and pantolactone formation are included, and discharge-derived norvaline and isovaline require analytical separation from valine. All environments are anoxic. Surface chemistry and metal hydrogenation are separate implementations: cyanide- and sulfide-rich photochemical effluent is not assumed compatible with hydrogenation catalysts. Kaur et al. (2024) conditions are experimental benchmarks; other numerical windows are explicitly proposed implementation or screening conditions unless stated otherwise. Hot nitrile hydrolysis, volatile retention, precursor concentration and branched-skeleton supply remain important bottlenecks. Equations use neutral-parent molecular bookkeeping, with acid-base speciation and subsequent neutralization implicit. Stoichiometric coefficients are stated in mechanism fields, never encoded by repeating molecule IDs. Composite equations are atom-balanced projections, not measured product ratios. Because the role vocabulary lacks 'byproduct', non-target terminal products are labeled 'intermediate'. The environment_formed field identifies a principal formation setting; reaction nodes specify alternative settings.\n\nMOLECULES (24 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2 (Hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C3H6O (Acetone) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C4H8O (Isobutyraldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C5H8O3 (Alpha-ketoisovaleric acid) [starting]\n  - mol_009: CH4 (Methane) [starting]\n  - mol_010: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_011: C4H9N (Isobutyraldehyde imine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H10N2 (Valine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C5H11NO2 (Valine) [target]\n  - mol_014: C5H9NO (Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C4H7NO (Acetone cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C4H9NOS (Acetone-cyanohydrin-derived hydroxythioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C5H10O3 (Alpha-hydroxyisovaleric acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C5H10O2 (Hydroxypivaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C6H11NO2 (Pantoic acid nitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C6H12O4 (Pan