  \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) provide direct support for valine formation using ground Aletai iron meteorite. Extending this result to unspecified exposed meteoritic Fe-Ni requires evidence about accessible surface area, weathering and passivation. The supplied alpha-ketoisovalerate boundary is explicit and chemically legitimate. Competition with hydroxy-acid formation is represented correctly, although its magnitude under the proposed implementation is unknown.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify whether the implementation uses ground meteorite matching the experiment or intact exposed material; measure activity for the actual particle size and surface condition.\",\n        \"Document the aqueous H2 supply, pressure retention, ammonium concentration and catalyst lifetime.\",\n        \"Quantify valine versus alpha-hydroxyisovalerate instead of assuming the selectivity of supported palladium.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.89,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.75,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.94,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.87,\n      \"feedback\": \"Ring et al. (1972) and the Miller-sample reanalyses support actual valine production in reducing-atmosphere discharge experiments. The balanced composite is a defensible summary of discharge chemistry plus aqueous workup, not an elementary reaction or evidence for equal isomer yields and quantitative carbon conversion. The three C5 isomers are properly distinguished. The main uncertainties are the exact gas composition, reducing atmospheric setting, energy delivery and hydrolysis protocol.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_019\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify ga